</node>
<node id="Syringe">
  <data key="d0">Syringe</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A syringe is a medical device primarily designed for the administration of insulin, serving as a critical tool in diabetes management by enabling accurate measurement and delivery of insulin doses. It functions by drawing insulin from a vial and injecting it subcutaneously, with proper technique requiring users to maintain pressure on the plunger during needle withdrawal to prevent leakage and ensure complete dose administration. Syringes are available in various types, capacities, and graduations—such as 100UI, 50UI, and 30UI—to accommodate individual patient needs and support precise dosing. The selection of the appropriate syringe size, along with correct injection angle and technique, is essential to optimize therapeutic effectiveness and minimize complications such as tissue damage or inconsistent absorption.

For patients who require mixed insulin regimens, syringes with fixed needles are recommended to ensure accurate mixing and administration of different insulin types. Specific syringe designs and graduations are emphasized to enhance both safety and dosing accuracy, underscoring the importance of proper device selection. Beyond administration, correct handling, usage, and disposal of syringes are vital to prevent contamination, needlestick injuries, and the risks associated with reuse. Comprehensive patient education on syringe selection, injection technique, and safe disposal practices is integral to effective insulin therapy and overall patient safety.&lt;SEP&gt;A syringe is a medical device used to prepare and administer insulin, particularly when graduated in milliliters, which can lead to dosing errors if not properly matched to insulin concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;The syringe is an alternative device for insulin injection, less convenient than the insulin pen, and typically associated with longer needles.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical device used to draw and inject insulin doses, requiring precise handling to avoid dosage errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea5432a4b376d5a48f31d6694c76d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54575ceb6b15fc8abcc2afcec7b36792&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afd148f1edc7929cecea8e4d981ed04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c897fc79a7dde2715e95be7ca23f57f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb18b1ccdbe7c3a6bbce149470c1ca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-180dc3d244f7d832cf505b09cfc34746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6a188926df383900f31928c8726a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb4a4dec39574c303988e93b87c351d4</data>
