</node>
<node id="Injection Site">
  <data key="d0">Injection Site</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection Site refers to specific areas on the human body where insulin is administered via subcutaneous injection, playing a critical role in effective diabetes management. Commonly recommended locations include the abdomen, thighs, arms, flanks, and buttocks, all of which possess adequate subcutaneous fat layers necessary for consistent insulin absorption. The abdomen is particularly favored due to its rapid and predictable absorption rates, while sites such as the thighs and arms may offer slightly slower absorption, influencing the timing and effectiveness of insulin action. These anatomical regions are selected not only for their accessibility and safety but also for their reliable pharmacokinetic profiles, ensuring stable blood glucose control.

Proper management of injection sites involves systematic rotation across and within these areas to prevent complications such as lipohypertrophy (thickened tissue), lipoatrophy (tissue atrophy), and localized damage, all of which can impair insulin absorption and contribute to glycemic variability. Repeated injections in the same location increase the risk of such complications, making site rotation a cornerstone of clinical guidelines. Additionally, patient factors such as body composition can affect the depth of injection, with individuals having less subcutaneous fat at higher risk of accidental intramuscular delivery, potentially altering insulin onset and increasing hypoglycemia risk.

Beyond rotation, injection sites require careful inspection and preparation before each use to ensure skin integrity and optimal insulin delivery. Variations in absorption and patient-specific conditions—such as age, weight, and metabolic state—may influence the choice of site, allowing for individualized treatment approaches. Some descriptions also note the possibility of insulin loss post-injection at the site, further emphasizing the importance of correct technique. Collectively, these factors underscore that Injection Site selection and care are integral components of insulin therapy, necessitating patient education on anatomical considerations, rotation practices, and proper injection procedures to achieve therapeutic efficacy and safety.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03a1de93a53fbcb513268c123e2c5a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fee4c8681a7629eb6ff0eb2470a98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c897fc79a7dde2715e95be7ca23f57f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78692c868b2064d1fbeaf7aec1c44948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6a188926df383900f31928c8726a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a04357a03a35f68e0068d23b80593b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bbc7b2df438b8290a4da57f63f1ff5d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
