</node>
<node id="Needle Reuse">
  <data key="d0">Needle Reuse</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle Reuse refers to the practice of using the same insulin injection needle for multiple administrations, a behavior commonly observed among individuals managing diabetes despite being strongly discouraged by medical authorities. Insulin pen needles are designed for single use, and reusing them—particularly beyond three injections—compromises needle integrity, leading to dulling, bending, and increased tissue trauma during injection. This practice is widely recognized as unsafe due to its association with heightened pain, localized infections, inflammation, and skin damage. Medical guidelines consistently emphasize that needle reuse violates sterile injection protocols and poses significant risks to both immediate and long-term health, undermining effective diabetes management.

A major complication linked to Needle Reuse is lipodystrophy, specifically lipo-hypertrophy, which involves abnormal fat accumulation at repeated injection sites. Reusing needles more than five times substantially increases the likelihood of developing this condition. Areas affected by lipo-hypertrophy often exhibit reduced pain sensitivity, making injections feel less painful and potentially reinforcing the habit of reuse. This diminished discomfort can mislead individuals into perceiving needle reuse as safe or beneficial, thereby perpetuating a harmful cycle that compromises skin health and insulin absorption. Consequently, the practice not only damages tissue but also impairs metabolic control by affecting the consistency and effectiveness of insulin delivery.

Although some individuals may reuse needles for reasons of convenience or cost-saving, clinical evidence shows that these perceived benefits are outweighed by the risks to treatment efficacy and overall health. Needle Reuse is consistently associated with increased risks of infection, pain, and complications such as lipohypertrophy. However, structured patient education on proper insulin administration techniques has been shown to significantly reduce the prevalence of this behavior. These interventions highlight the importance of regular needle replacement and affirm that Needle Reuse is a preventable risk factor in diabetes care.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle reuse refers to the practice of using the same insulin injection needle multiple times, which increases the risk of developing lipo-hypertrophy.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle Reuse é um fator predisponente para o desenvolvimento de lipohipertrofia, relacionado à prática de reutilizar agulhas durante injeções de insulina.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d9f7234865211afc5d8343294b276d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7740b86f0b3f110bb2c3f3d05e4da41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8abf9336f83edf5c92071da1032dc483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b2243846b349d5b78af57a8e77b6ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a04357a03a35f68e0068d23b80593b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f8c8b2b40633cf37993b620002b889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-202f1dba3a8254f9d5867b459cdbcfa2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/O que você precisa saber sobre Lipohipertrofia - Sociedade Brasileira de Diabetes.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776640761</data>
