</node>
<node id="Hemoglobina Glicada">
  <data key="d0">Hemoglobina Glicada</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemoglobina Glicada, commonly known as HbA1c, is a critical biomarker used in clinical practice to measure average blood glucose levels over an extended period, typically reflecting glycemic control over the past two to three months. This blood test is based on the non-enzymatic glycation of hemoglobin within red blood cells, with higher glucose concentrations leading to increased glycation. As such, Hemoglobina Glicada serves as a reliable indicator of long-term glucose exposure.

Hemoglobina Glicada (HbA1c) plays a dual role in diabetes management: it is used both for the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus and for monitoring the effectiveness of glycemic control in individuals already diagnosed with the condition. According to established clinical guidelines, a diagnosis of diabetes can be made when HbA1c levels are equal to or greater than 6.5%. This diagnostic threshold allows for a standardized, convenient, and accurate method of identifying diabetes without requiring fasting or oral glucose tolerance tests.

In addition to its diagnostic utility, Hemoglobina Glicada is instrumental in evaluating the success of various diabetes treatment strategies, including therapeutic approaches such as sensor-augmented insulin pump therapy (SICI) and multiple daily insulin injections (MDI). By providing an integrated measure of blood glucose levels, HbA1c enables healthcare providers to assess the long-term impact of interventions and adjust treatment plans accordingly. Repeated measurements over time help track progress and support clinical decisions aimed at reducing the risk of diabetes-related complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobina Glicada (HbA1c) is a blood test measuring average blood glucose levels over time, used for diagnosing diabetes when levels are equal to or greater than 6.5%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f739b5aa260687129c0a88dce08d70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19e4dac1289de717e1280645c07b7a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd7841f818e5dcdd6ef3c0c2951cf8a0</data>
