</node>
<node id="Diabetes Mellitus Type 2">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2, also known as DM2, is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by elevated blood glucose levels due to insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency. It arises when the body becomes resistant to insulin or fails to produce enough insulin to maintain normal glucose homeostasis, distinguishing it from Type 1 diabetes, which results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells. In contrast, individuals with Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 often retain some beta-cell function and exhibit higher levels of C-peptide, indicating ongoing endogenous insulin production. The condition is strongly associated with obesity, sedentary lifestyle, genetic predisposition, and family history, traditionally affecting adults but increasingly diagnosed in children and adolescents, reflecting a growing public health concern.

Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 is a primary focus of screening, diagnosis, and prevention efforts due to its rising global prevalence and complex interplay of genetic and environmental factors. It is frequently linked to insulin resistance, a hallmark feature that also appears in conditions like lipodystrophy, though the two differ in pathology, severity, and age of onset. Because of overlapping symptoms and metabolic profiles, DM2 can sometimes be difficult to differentiate from Type 1 diabetes, especially in atypical cases. The disorder is central to research on insulin delivery methods, particularly in adult populations, and management typically involves lifestyle interventions such as diet and exercise, along with pharmacological treatments including oral hypoglycemic agents and, when necessary, insulin therapy.

Despite being less common than in Type 1 diabetes, complications such as lipohipertrophy—localized fat accumulation at insulin injection sites—can occur in individuals with Diabetes Mellitus Type 2, especially those on long-term insulin treatment. As a major focus in clinical diabetes care, DM2 underscores the importance of early detection and comprehensive management strategies to mitigate long-term complications and address its increasing incidence across all age groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by insulin resistance and high blood glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus Type 2, also known as DM2, is a metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels and is the focus of several clinical studies on cardiovascular outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 (DM2) is a form of diabetes mentioned in the context of individualized glycemic targets for different patient groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fee4c8681a7629eb6ff0eb2470a98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-202f1dba3a8254f9d5867b459cdbcfa2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c072c5ad49b2fb7ef54c9a2fe121badb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed9228bfdd30fe45392a8a9fb8d106b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00f6ea581f76ddc15bc463d1eccc4496&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8631f035300b1b65337131c6c6079b19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f87c8aceb9340a61d8283da6282edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8707947fb493803b7f3759b0105eda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d8f51fe0c33fe28ed188c855a82a3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-309ce40527b1cc8f260e96e195742001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f7252ca9147660b04b7329edbe843f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ad6cd6b0bad77397aa6685ccb2cfaef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d39cd334b01ad820816bf3cb108c0e27</data>
