<node id="Hemoglobin A1c">
  <data key="d0">Hemoglobin A1c</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) is a blood test and clinical biomarker that measures the average level of blood glucose over the past two to three months, serving as a critical tool for assessing long-term glycemic control in individuals with diabetes. It reflects the degree to which glucose has become chemically bonded to hemoglobin in red blood cells, forming glycated hemoglobin, a process that occurs gradually and continuously in proportion to blood glucose concentration. Because red blood cells typically survive for about 120 days, the HbA1c level provides a stable and integrated measure of glucose exposure during this period, making it more reliable than daily glucose monitoring, which can be affected by short-term fluctuations.

Hemoglobin A1c is widely used in both clinical practice and research for the diagnosis, screening, and ongoing management of diabetes. It plays a central role in diagnosing type 2 diabetes and prediabetes in adults and children, with an HbA1c level of 6.5% or higher generally indicating diabetes, while levels between 5.7% and 6.4% suggest prediabetes. For individuals already diagnosed with diabetes, HbA1c is a key metric for evaluating the effectiveness of treatment strategies, including medication, diet, and lifestyle changes. A commonly recommended target for most adults is an HbA1c level below 7%, though individualized goals may vary based on factors such as age, comorbidities, and risk of hypoglycemia.

Elevated HbA1c levels are strongly associated with an increased risk of long-term diabetes complications, including neuropathy, retinopathy, nephropathy, and cardiovascular disease. The test does not require fasting, enhancing its convenience and utility in routine care. Additionally, advancements such as continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) have been shown to support improved glycemic outcomes, including reductions in HbA1c, by enabling real-time adjustments to therapy. As a cornerstone of diabetes management, Hemoglobin A1c remains an indispensable tool for guiding clinical decisions and improving patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c is a glycated hemoglobin protein used as a biomarker for average blood glucose levels over time and is central to diabetes diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c is a biomarker used for the diagnosis of type 2 diabetes and prediabetes in both adults and children.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c is a blood test that measures average blood glucose levels over the past 2–3 months and is used in diabetes screening and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) measures the average blood glucose level over the past three months, with higher values indicating higher average glucose levels; a common target is below 7%.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) is a blood test that reflects average blood glucose levels over the past 3 to 4 months and is used to monitor long-term glycemic control in diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c is a biomarker used to assess long-term blood glucose levels and is associated with mortality in older adults with and without diabetes.</data>
