<node id="Diabetes Type 1">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Type 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Type 1 is an autoimmune chronic metabolic disorder in which the body's immune system attacks and destroys the insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas, resulting in little or no endogenous insulin production. This complete or near-complete absence of insulin necessitates lifelong daily insulin therapy for survival, as individuals with Diabetes Type 1 cannot regulate blood glucose levels without exogenous insulin administration. The condition is often diagnosed in childhood or adolescence, though it can develop at any age, and requires continuous, comprehensive management to maintain glycemic control and prevent complications.

Due to the necessity of frequent insulin injections, individuals with Diabetes Type 1 are at increased risk of injection-related complications, particularly lipo-hypertrophy—an abnormal accumulation of fat tissue at repeated injection sites. This condition underscores the importance of proper injection techniques, site rotation, and regular monitoring of subcutaneous tissue health to ensure effective insulin absorption and minimize complications. Long-term management must therefore include not only insulin delivery but also patient education on injection practices and tissue care.

Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) has become a critical tool in the management of Diabetes Type 1, offering real-time glucose readings and trend alerts that support more precise insulin dosing. Given the frequent insulin administration and heightened risk of hypoglycemia associated with the condition, CGM systems significantly enhance glycemic control and reduce the incidence of acute complications. Together, insulin therapy, advanced monitoring technologies, and vigilant self-care form the cornerstone of effective, ongoing management for individuals living with Diabetes Type 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 1 is an autoimmune condition causing destruction of pancreatic beta cells, leading to severe insulin deficiency, often diagnosed in adulthood despite being common in children and adolescents.</data>
