  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Glucometer">
  <data key="d0">Glucometer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucometer is a medical device used to measure blood glucose levels by analyzing a small blood sample, typically obtained through a finger-prick or fingerstick method. It serves as a key tool in capillary blood glucose monitoring (AMGC), where the blood sample is applied to a disposable test strip that is then inserted into the device for analysis. The glucometer detects the glucose concentration through a chemical reaction on the test strip, delivering a quantitative and immediate reading of blood sugar levels.

The device plays a central role in traditional glucose monitoring, particularly for individuals managing diabetes. It has historically been essential for confirming blood glucose readings obtained from continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems before insulin administration, ensuring treatment decisions are based on accurate data. Despite advancements in CGM technology, the glucometer remains a reliable, widely used instrument for point-of-care testing due to its precision and accessibility.

Multiple descriptions consistently highlight the glucometer’s core functionality: analyzing blood samples via test strips to provide actionable results. There are no conflicting accounts among the provided sources; all emphasize its use of a small blood sample, reliance on test strips, and application in both home and clinical settings for effective diabetes management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6828aacfba0c2a6ae59c439a4e4b161&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79d0e4d0f656bd87d2d0c4657378db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f2616cb245736f951a3dcbf261b3b9b</data>
