<node id="Tecido Subcutâneo">
  <data key="d0">Tecido Subcutâneo</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Tecido Subcutâneo is the layer of fatty tissue located directly beneath the skin, serving as the primary site for insulin administration in individuals managing diabetes. This anatomical layer is composed mainly of adipose tissue and connective elements, providing both insulation and energy storage, while also acting as a crucial medium for subcutaneous drug delivery. Its vascularization and composition allow for consistent and controlled absorption of insulin, which is essential for maintaining stable blood glucose levels.

The importance of administering insulin into the Tecido Subcutâneo lies in its ability to ensure proper absorption kinetics and therapeutic effectiveness. Injecting insulin into this layer helps prevent complications such as intramuscular delivery, which can lead to faster and less predictable absorption, increasing the risk of hypoglycemia. Multiple descriptions emphasize that correct injection technique into the Tecido Subcutâneo is vital to achieve optimal glycemic control and minimize adverse effects.

Repeatedly highlighted across both Portuguese and English descriptions, the role of Tecido Subcutâneo as the recommended site for insulin delivery underscores its clinical significance in diabetes care. Whether described as "camada de tecido logo abaixo da pele" or "fatty tissue layer beneath the skin," the consensus confirms its function as the ideal target for subcutaneous injections due to its reliable absorption properties. Overall, Tecido Subcutâneo is consistently recognized as a critical anatomical site for effective insulin therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tecido subcutâneo é a camada de tecido sob a pele onde a cânula do sistema de infusão é inserida para administração contínua de insulina.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebdb9452013f0e340884677832714553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e490a7e963a20e2221a8097b50073543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-780053d90e5c36ef6ea68e1a598cc53b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8792cad0fa7c0226f159b670b4e743a1</data>
