<node id="Enzyme">
  <data key="d0">Enzyme</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Enzyme is a substance present on the test strip used in blood glucose monitoring systems that plays a critical role in measuring blood sugar levels. It functions by reacting specifically with glucose in a blood sample, initiating the biochemical process necessary for glucose detection. This reaction is the foundational step in the measurement process, enabling the system to quantify glucose concentration accurately.

The enzymatic reaction generates an electrical signal proportional to the amount of glucose present in the sample. This electrical signal is then detected and interpreted by the glucometer, which converts it into a numerical glucose reading displayed to the user. The consistent and reliable performance of the enzyme ensures the accuracy and efficiency of blood glucose monitoring, making it an essential component of diabetes management devices. Multiple descriptions emphasize both the initiation of the glucose measurement process and the generation of an electrical signal, indicating that these are complementary aspects of the enzyme’s function within the test strip system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638269</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
