  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lipoatrophy">
  <data key="d0">Lipoatrophy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipoatrophy is a rare form of lipodystrophy characterized by the progressive loss of subcutaneous fat tissue, typically occurring at insulin injection or infusion sites in individuals with diabetes. This condition manifests clinically as visible skin depressions, holes, or well-demarcated indentations at the affected sites, resulting from localized atrophy of adipose tissue. The primary cause is repeated insulin administration in the same area without adequate rotation of injection sites, which can trigger an immune or inflammatory response leading to fat breakdown. These tissue changes not only affect cosmetic appearance but also impair insulin absorption, causing irregular pharmacokinetics and potentially contributing to poor glycemic control, including episodes of hyperglycemia.

Multiple descriptions in the input conflate lipoatrophy with lipo-hypertrophy, a distinct condition involving the accumulation of fatty tissue and the formation of lumps or nodules at injection sites. However, clinical evidence clearly differentiates the two: lipoatrophy involves fat loss, while lipo-hypertrophy involves fat gain. Despite this frequent confusion in terminology—evident in descriptions that incorrectly equate lipoatrophy with nodule or lump formation—the correct pathological definition of lipoatrophy is adipose atrophy. The repeated mislabeling of lipo-hypertrophic changes as lipoatrophy across several sources appears to stem from overlapping risk factors and clinical consequences, such as impaired insulin absorption and the need for site rotation, but does not justify the conflation of the two entities.

Early detection of lipoatrophy is essential and can be achieved through routine physical examination of injection sites during clinical visits. Once identified, insulin should no longer be administered in affected areas to prevent further tissue damage and absorption variability. Prevention remains the cornerstone of management, emphasizing patient education on proper injection techniques, including systematic rotation of injection sites and avoidance of reuse of needles. With the advent of newer, purer insulin formulations, the incidence of lipoatrophy has decreased significantly, but it remains a relevant concern, particularly in settings where insulin quality or patient education is suboptimal. Overall, lipoatrophy is a preventable complication of insulin therapy that underscores the importance of adherence to best practices in diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipoatrophy, or lipo-hypertrophy, refers to the formation of lumps under the skin from repeated insulin injections in the same spot, which can be avoided by rotating injection sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipoatrophy is a less common skin condition involving fat loss at insulin injection or pump sites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51960dcc8f6bd8e9d62d3512fa1e35af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b2243846b349d5b78af57a8e77b6ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f8c8b2b40633cf37993b620002b889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679c193ea4fd33e94141ac2c9fef69c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f357ebc59eb6ed3dd2035a13da0df52f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643692</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lipohypertrophy">
  <data key="d0">Lipohypertrophy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipohypertrophy is a form of lipodystrophy characterized by abnormal fat accumulation at insulin injection or infusion sites, resulting in the development of hardened nodules, lumps, or thickened areas beneath the skin. It is the most common type of lipodystrophy observed in individuals with diabetes who administer repeated subcutaneous insulin injections. The condition arises primarily due to the frequent use of the same injection site and the reuse of needles, both of which contribute to localized tissue trauma and subsequent fat buildup.

These tissue changes impair insulin absorption by altering the predictable diffusion and uptake of insulin from the subcutaneous layer, often leading to delayed or erratic insulin action. As a result, lipohypertrophy can significantly compromise glycemic control, contributing to unexplained fluctuations in blood glucose levels and increasing the risk of both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia. The irregular absorption of insulin from affected sites undermines treatment effectiveness and complicates diabetes management.

Clinical studies have shown that the incidence of lipohypertrophy can be reduced through proper injection technique and patient education. Patients who receive structured training on site rotation and the importance of using new needles for each injection are less likely to develop the condition. Regular self-examination and clinical assessment of injection sites are recommended to detect early signs of tissue changes and prevent progression. By promoting awareness and adherence to best injection practices, healthcare providers can help minimize the occurrence of lipohypertrophy and its adverse effects on diabetes outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipohypertrophy is a skin condition caused by repeated insulin injections or pump site use, leading to fatty tissue buildup.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipohypertrophy is a condition observed in young patients with type 1 diabetes, associated with insulin injection sites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea5432a4b376d5a48f31d6694c76d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7740b86f0b3f110bb2c3f3d05e4da41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b2243846b349d5b78af57a8e77b6ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dde6c55191737188788dc809811837cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679c193ea4fd33e94141ac2c9fef69c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdbe9f47931df265e815faee2c67f81b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f357ebc59eb6ed3dd2035a13da0df52f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643670</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Injection Site Rotation">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Injection Site Rotation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin injection site rotation is a recommended practice to prevent lipodystrophy by varying the anatomical location of injections.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin injection site rotation is a structured practice of changing the location of insulin injections to prevent complications such as lipohypertrophy and to maintain consistent insulin absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin injection site rotation is a structured practice of changing the location of insulin injections to prevent complications such as lipohypertrophy and to maintain consistent insulin absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin injection site rotation is the practice of systematically changing the location of insulin injections to prevent tissue damage and complications such as lipo-hypertrophy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638262</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Storage">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Storage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Storage refers to the comprehensive set of conditions and practices necessary to preserve the stability, safety, and therapeutic efficacy of insulin for diabetes management. Proper storage begins with unopened insulin, which must be refrigerated at a controlled temperature between 2°C and 8°C, ideally stored above the vegetable drawer to avoid exposure to colder zones near freezer walls. It must never be frozen, as freezing renders insulin ineffective and unsafe for use. Direct contact with ice packs or placement in the freezer compartment is strictly prohibited. During transport, unopened insulin should be carried in insulated packaging within carry-on luggage, using cooling methods that prevent direct ice contact to maintain temperature integrity.

Once opened, insulin—whether in vials or pens—can be stored at room temperature, typically not exceeding 30°C, for a period of up to 30 to 45 days, depending on the manufacturer’s guidelines. During this in-use phase, it should be protected from excessive heat, direct sunlight, and physical agitation. All insulin types follow the general principle of refrigeration for unopened units and room temperature storage for opened ones. To ensure safe usage, insulin should be labeled with the opening date, and stored separately from other injectables such as heparin and vaccines to prevent medication errors. A designated, light-resistant plastic container is recommended for organizing in-use insulin and shielding it from environmental stressors.

Overall, Insulin Storage encompasses strict temperature control, appropriate handling, environmental protection, and adherence to product-specific instructions. These protocols ensure that insulin remains effective throughout its usable life, supporting reliable and safe diabetes treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin storage involves keeping unopened insulin refrigerated and opened insulin in a cool place away from sunlight for up to 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin storage requires proper conditions: unopened vials or pens should be refrigerated between 2°C and 8°C, while opened ones can be kept at room temperature, away from heat and sunlight, for up to 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin storage involves keeping unopened insulin refrigerated and opened insulin in a cool place away from sunlight for up to 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin storage requires proper conditions: unopened vials or pens should be refrigerated between 2°C and 8°C, while opened ones can be kept at room temperature, away from heat and sunlight, for up to 30 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54575ceb6b15fc8abcc2afcec7b36792&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03a1de93a53fbcb513268c123e2c5a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d9f7234865211afc5d8343294b276d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e68da6d45eb01c249e854b711007d805&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afd148f1edc7929cecea8e4d981ed04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51960dcc8f6bd8e9d62d3512fa1e35af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dde6c55191737188788dc809811837cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c96e22adae6d8c02ec9ce8430d0cf64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a04357a03a35f68e0068d23b80593b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11ac81b4dee4dbb3636b8dc2403e0661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8702ff00244abf3caabafff5aad4a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e94cedc7134adbb256c931b851235c53</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas FRASCOS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Armazenamento e Transporte/Cuidados com a Insulinoterapia conservar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Armazenamento e Transporte/transporte e conservação Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-20201.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776641984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Refrigerated Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Refrigerated Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">substance</data>
  <data key="d2">Refrigerated insulin is insulin stored at 2–8°C and was associated with a 30% lower glucose infusion rate compared to non-refrigerated insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Refrigerated insulin is stored at 2–8°C and shows faster absorption and earlier peak plasma levels when injected, compared to insulin stored at room temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7740b86f0b3f110bb2c3f3d05e4da41</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776637660</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Room Temperature Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Room Temperature Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">substance</data>
  <data key="d2">Room temperature insulin is insulin in use, stored at up to 30°C, resulting in slower and more prolonged absorption compared to refrigerated insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776637661</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type 1 Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 1 Diabetes is a chronic autoimmune condition characterized by the immune system's destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas, resulting in an absolute deficiency of insulin. This leads to the body's inability to regulate blood glucose levels, necessitating lifelong exogenous insulin therapy for survival. The condition often presents with a rapid onset and can cause acute complications such as diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) and significantly elevated blood glucose levels, particularly if diagnosis is delayed. Before the discovery of insulin, Type 1 Diabetes was typically fatal within a year of diagnosis, but with modern treatment, survival is possible through continuous and intensive management to prevent both acute and long-term complications.

Although most commonly diagnosed in children, adolescents, and young adults, Type 1 Diabetes can develop at any age, including in adults over 30. In such cases, it is frequently misclassified as type 2 diabetes, highlighting the importance of accurate diagnostic methods. The detection of islet autoantibodies—biomarkers of autoimmune activity—is critical for distinguishing Type 1 Diabetes from other forms of diabetes. The presence of multiple autoantibodies can indicate a preclinical phase and increased risk of disease development, supporting early screening and intervention to prevent complications like DKA. Latent autoimmune diabetes of adulthood (LADA) is recognized as part of the Type 1 Diabetes spectrum due to its autoimmune etiology and progressive loss of beta-cell function.

Management of Type 1 Diabetes centers on intensive insulin therapy, delivered through multiple daily injections or insulin delivery systems such as insulin pumps and automated insulin delivery devices. These are increasingly integrated with continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) to enable real-time glucose tracking, reduce the risk of hypoglycemia, and support precise insulin dosing. Advances in digital health technologies, including closed-loop and artificial pancreas systems, are the focus of ongoing research aimed at improving glycemic control and quality of life. Despite these innovations, exogenous insulin remains essential, as endogenous insulin production is absent. Research continues to explore screening strategies, preventive therapies, and potential disease-modifying interventions to alter the progression of this lifelong condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune form of diabetes studied among children and adolescents, often compared with type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 Diabetes is an autoimmune condition where the body fails to produce insulin, often diagnosed in children and young adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition where the body attacks insulin-producing cells, leading to insulin deficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Type 1 diabetes is a form of diabetes mellitus commonly diagnosed in children and adolescents, requiring insulin therapy and careful glycemic monitoring to manage blood glucose levels and reduce complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2bb9a343108d712fe87613f1b48d18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54930ff88c0a13a8693f1c1b3407ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182332eb7daaa8deed5e90a79db308e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8d6904368d45b0f9532b36061a00e7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bddc99b2e70c378921ff59ddb3b954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7fb8feaa2febe2b5f8525b93f3080f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5647e96fec802433abcc8922a33d23b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6764f5f0af1c9855a1901ea1bef131d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e08a876cbf5eb18e5ec55068b1a54d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08c7f7bb0209cabd75e27f9c8556c214&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f4e05859597c5f1693c2f9fe1ef0a02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-755e9677a7018e4cc382b7d903590786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b94e98f4cb6f97fceae5e6d8c30c871b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1858f15899659bc0116977a6e1e000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0a49a3e1aba9780862b94a266490691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-418c63d7f8a7a0408d9107e40d9251db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0cc6a5cedffa24f9a3134770cf1526b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdddb31a1876a8809b8555564982acd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36191c270d1cc8ff8c1fc610d7a533b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1eea5927c2474e7c5774b94980fc8b78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdbe9f47931df265e815faee2c67f81b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce5b147555a2c6ba2644e98fb3d31f6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1319c06eac1875d14b345f09579021be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07c7e3d6615d1076ae20653358419ff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f357ebc59eb6ed3dd2035a13da0df52f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1f5c2bbe2ca0e80b85850ca2f9a5c84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1af4e8af9934463f38c5951016d9664b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aebc875b323050c4274610952a881b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c37422889250d7e58837deb17406d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f5cd98bab9909442c09e3fcd5b21fda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620e2d1316d6093170f1c9991caabc7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa8638b27cbd181b507c81fea49c6f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3bdf442194477639404123cea5d5974&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0510d1781e9c07bebd7a7422d453cbf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c47356c03c704ef89b9f41bbd939bc8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05070e0755e36e3375d401400cf75fce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f76e40d0d9b4211d8c61a53ea298d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb7f10473b84f2de23852febe7ef317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cc90e1dea7f4271dde1545434c62508&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e35953dbfdebbbab4bf83ec032ccfb7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a109575f50250430ade32316446ee9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12999315830b302d973dd1908eab1e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02fb9dc2bae2939b212aa5df09194ee4</data>
