  <data key="d5">1776638260</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brazil">
  <data key="d0">Brazil</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Brazil is a country where diabetes care and management are shaped by a combination of regulatory frameworks, healthcare access challenges, and evolving medical technologies. The country adheres to specific technical and legal guidelines for insulin waste disposal, reflecting a structured approach to managing diabetes-related medical waste. In clinical practice, U100 insulin and corresponding syringes are the standard for diabetes treatment, and the available insulin formulations are formally listed in national health data, indicating a regulated supply system.

Access to diabetes monitoring tools within Brazil's public health system remains limited, particularly regarding blood ketone test strips, for which availability is restricted and standardized guidance is lacking. Despite these challenges, Brazil has seen advancements in diabetes technology, with various insulin pump systems either currently available or expected to become commercialized. Notably, the Accu-Chek patch pump is anticipated to launch in the country soon, signaling a regional expansion in diabetes management technologies and improved options for patients.

Brazil also serves as a site for research and implementation of diabetes screening, diagnosis, and management protocols. Studies on diabetes screening have been conducted, and established guidelines are applied in clinical settings. However, in the area of type 1 diabetes prevention, Brazil currently has no proven effective interventions to delay progression to stage 3, and no immunomodulatory medications for type 1 diabetes have been approved by ANVISA, the national health regulatory agency. This highlights a gap in advanced therapeutic options despite ongoing efforts in diabetes care and innovation.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil is a country where studies on diabetes screening and diagnosis have been conducted, and where guidelines for diabetes management are applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil is a country where there is currently no proven effective intervention available for delaying type 1 diabetes stage 3, and no immunomodulatory medications approved by ANVISA.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88336f998ca464e0ed477249555b3e44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8085de73bd4812f966c059205e5d3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed992e01da83317a2c9f0adf751f53f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dad8318811ce10b7f639b13057d611f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702</data>
