</node>
<node id="Subcutaneous Fold">
  <data key="d0">Subcutaneous Fold</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Subcutaneous Fold is a technique employed during insulin administration to ensure accurate delivery of insulin into the subcutaneous tissue, the fatty layer beneath the skin. It involves gently pinching the skin using the thumb and forefinger to elevate a fold of skin and underlying fat, thereby creating a thicker layer of subcutaneous tissue at the injection site. This method is particularly important in preventing inadvertent intramuscular injection, which can alter insulin absorption rates and potentially affect glycemic control.

The need for a subcutaneous fold is influenced by factors such as needle length, injection site, and patient-specific characteristics including body mass and skin thickness. By lifting the fatty layer, the technique reduces the risk of the needle penetrating into muscle tissue, especially when shorter needles are not used or when injecting in areas with less subcutaneous fat. Multiple descriptions consistently emphasize that the primary purpose of the subcutaneous fold is to isolate the subcutaneous tissue and enhance the safety and efficacy of insulin delivery. This practice is widely recommended in clinical guidelines for individuals managing diabetes through injectable insulin therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e68da6d45eb01c249e854b711007d805&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c897fc79a7dde2715e95be7ca23f57f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b2243846b349d5b78af57a8e77b6ff5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776639027</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hand Hygiene">
  <data key="d0">Hand Hygiene</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hand hygiene involves washing and drying hands before insulin preparation and administration to prevent infection.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of washing and drying hands before insulin preparation to reduce infection risk and maintain aseptic conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78692c868b2064d1fbeaf7aec1c44948</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776639046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cotton">
  <data key="d0">Cotton</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cotton is a material commonly utilized in the insulin injection process for various purposes related to hygiene and post-injection care. It is frequently employed to clean the rubber stopper of an insulin vial prior to use, typically in conjunction with alcohol, to ensure the injection site remains sterile and free from contaminants. This step is critical in preventing infection and maintaining the integrity of the medication.

In addition to vial preparation, cotton plays a role in the administration process itself. It is used to wipe the skin at the injection site after disinfection with alcohol, helping to remove any residual antiseptic and prepare the area for needle insertion. This practice supports cleanliness and reduces the risk of irritation or reaction at the injection site.

Following insulin administration, cotton is also applied to compress the injection site, typically for about five seconds. This compression aids in preventing bleeding or leakage of insulin from the puncture site, promoting better absorption and minimizing waste of medication. Multiple descriptions confirm this post-injection use, emphasizing cotton’s function in managing minor bleeding and ensuring a clean, controlled conclusion to the injection process. Overall, cotton serves as a versatile and essential component in the safe and effective delivery of insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Cotton is used to compress the injection site for 5 seconds after insulin administration.&lt;SEP&gt;Cotton is used to compress the injection site after needle removal to prevent bleeding or leakage.&lt;SEP&gt;Cotton is used to apply alcohol to the injection site for disinfection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622f3feb1f15564393e91cefc71256ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-780053d90e5c36ef6ea68e1a598cc53b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e68da6d45eb01c249e854b711007d805&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afd148f1edc7929cecea8e4d981ed04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c897fc79a7dde2715e95be7ca23f57f5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas FRASCOS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638656</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Puncture-Proof Container">
  <data key="d0">Puncture-Proof Container</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A puncture-proof container is a rigid, safety-compliant receptacle used for the safe disposal of sharps such as used needles and syringes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-035472811a4a16d98e62eba23a86dceb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776637675</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lipohipertrofia">
  <data key="d0">Lipohipertrofia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipohipertrofia is a skin condition associated with insulin therapy, specifically observed in diabetic patients who administer repeated insulin injections. It is characterized by the abnormal accumulation of fat tissue at injection sites, resulting in the formation of lumps or thickened areas in the skin. This condition arises primarily when insulin is consistently injected into the same location without adequate site rotation.

The development of Lipohipertrofia is directly linked to localized fat deposition triggered by the lipogenic properties of insulin. These subcutaneous lumps not only alter the physical appearance of the skin but also interfere with the effective absorption of insulin. As a result, insulin delivery becomes unpredictable, leading to impaired glycemic control and potential fluctuations in blood glucose levels.

Multiple descriptions consistently emphasize that Lipohipertrofia impairs insulin absorption and complicates diabetes management. Prevention strategies focus on proper injection techniques, including regular rotation of injection sites to avoid repeated trauma to the same area. Early recognition and patient education are critical to minimizing the occurrence of Lipohipertrofia and maintaining optimal therapeutic outcomes in individuals undergoing insulin treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipohipertrofia é um efeito colateral comum em pacientes submetidos à terapia insulínica de longo prazo, caracterizada por lesões fibrosas e pobremente vascularizadas no tecido adiposo subcutâneo.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8fc83e266bdeb54c4139a50adf2b793a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35a8ffab5eb63600b93fb2c67160b5ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708e3c514f667dc9979cfa8df875e473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-202f1dba3a8254f9d5867b459cdbcfa2</data>
