<node id="Insulin Therapy">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Therapy is a medical treatment used to manage blood glucose levels in individuals with diabetes by administering exogenous insulin, particularly when the body either fails to produce sufficient insulin or cannot effectively utilize the insulin it produces. First made possible by the discovery of insulin in 1922, this therapy is a cornerstone of diabetes management across various forms of the disease. It is essential for individuals with type 1 diabetes, who lack endogenous insulin production, and for those with advanced type 2 diabetes, where insulin resistance or deficiency necessitates supplemental insulin. Insulin Therapy is also indicated in specific populations, including young children, pregnant individuals, transplant recipients, patients with neonatal diabetes, and those with cystic fibrosis-related diabetes, where it supports metabolic stability and improves outcomes such as body mass index (BMI).

The therapy involves subcutaneous administration via injections or insulin pumps, with regimens designed to mimic natural insulin secretion through basal and prandial coverage. Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion through pumps or multiple daily injections allows for tight glycemic control, though proper technique, site rotation, and device management are critical to prevent complications such as lipohipertrophy. In pregnancy, insulin is the preferred pharmacological treatment for both pre-existing and gestational diabetes because it does not cross the placenta, thereby reducing risks such as macrosomia, preterm birth, and neonatal hypoglycemia. Similarly, in transplant patients, insulin therapy is the standard for managing hyperglycemia, post-transplant diabetes mellitus (PTDM), and preexisting diabetes, particularly in acute care settings.

Frequent blood glucose monitoring is a key component of effective insulin therapy, as it guides dosing adjustments and helps prevent complications such as hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia. In patients with rare conditions like lipodystrophy—characterized by abnormal fat distribution and severe insulin resistance—insulin therapy is often required early in the disease course and typically involves high doses, though subcutaneous fat deficiency complicates delivery and absorption. While insulin remains the primary treatment for neonatal diabetes, genetic testing may allow some patients to transition to sulfonylureas. Additionally, a potential complication of long-term insulin therapy is the development of antibodies against exogenous insulin, which may affect efficacy in rare cases. Despite these challenges, insulin therapy remains a versatile and indispensable intervention in achieving glycemic control across diverse clinical contexts.</data>
