<node id="A1C">
  <data key="d0">A1C</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7740b86f0b3f110bb2c3f3d05e4da41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c03aef84b96134fc1f0a2a4d8466e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdddb31a1876a8809b8555564982acd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36191c270d1cc8ff8c1fc610d7a533b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f12d6a3cebf5c39bed5629c359502cae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f357ebc59eb6ed3dd2035a13da0df52f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1f5c2bbe2ca0e80b85850ca2f9a5c84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6b2f90b1ec83426a0df84d7693bc26d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20b3e88dda40e2d303af38aea0d19b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53f409a49620f4d40ad687bd0c0df19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f182d57176633ee05e1a23031d6ad2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c85dba4b510b2463c6766fd8a2dbf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3bdf442194477639404123cea5d5974&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c47356c03c704ef89b9f41bbd939bc8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d936160aac8a6b8ba40b196280422fe5</data>
  <data key="d2">A1C, also known as glycated hemoglobin, is a blood test that measures average blood glucose levels over the past 2–3 months and serves as a key clinical indicator of long-term glycemic control. It reflects average plasma glucose concentrations and is widely used in the diagnosis, screening, and management of diabetes and prediabetes. An A1C level of 6.5% or higher, measured using NGSP-certified methods, is a diagnostic criterion for diabetes, while levels between 5.9% and 6.4% (41–47 mmol/mol) indicate an increased risk for developing diabetes or prediabetes. The test is particularly valuable because A1C levels can rise before the onset of clinical diabetes, making it a useful biomarker for early detection of abnormal glucose metabolism and for predicting future diabetes risk.

A1C plays a central role in both clinical practice and research, serving as a primary or secondary outcome in diabetes studies. It is used to monitor treatment efficacy, with interventions such as structured diabetes training and insulin pump therapy shown to significantly reduce A1C levels compared to control groups or multiple daily insulin injections. The test is also employed to screen for type 2 diabetes and to identify individuals at high risk for post-transplant diabetes mellitus (PTDM), often in conjunction with fasting glucose measurements. Despite its broad utility, A1C has limitations in specific populations. It is not recommended for screening cystic fibrosis–related diabetes due to low sensitivity, and it is not reliable for diagnosing gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) after 15 weeks of gestation. Although A1C testing during 24–28 weeks of pregnancy has been evaluated as a potential GDM screening tool, it is less effective than glucose tolerance tests (GLT). Nonetheless, A1C remains a cornerstone in diabetes care, providing essential insights into long-term glucose control, disease risk, and therapeutic outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647281</data>
