<node id="Insulin Pens">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Pens</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Pens are medical devices designed for the administration of insulin, primarily used by individuals managing diabetes. These portable injection devices offer a convenient and discreet method for self-administering insulin, often preferred over traditional vials and syringes due to their ease of use, dosing accuracy, and portability. They are especially beneficial for people with dexterity or vision impairments, as the design simplifies dose measurement and injection. 

Insulin Pens typically require the use of disposable needles, which must be properly attached before each use and safely discarded afterward to prevent contamination and ensure hygiene. The pens themselves may be either reusable or disposable; reusable pens are designed to accept insulin cartridges that can be replaced when empty, while disposable pens are pre-filled and intended for single-patient use until exhausted, after which the entire device must be disposed of properly. 

Across multiple descriptions, a consistent emphasis is placed on the importance of proper handling and disposal of both the needles and the pens, particularly for disposable models, to maintain safety and prevent environmental hazards. Clinical studies have compared Insulin Pens with vial-and-syringe methods, highlighting patient preference for pens due to their convenience, reduced injection pain, and improved adherence to treatment regimens. Overall, Insulin Pens represent a significant advancement in diabetes care, supporting effective insulin delivery with enhanced user experience.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e68da6d45eb01c249e854b711007d805&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de69fd058597c808e8be5bd334395b84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0a49a3e1aba9780862b94a266490691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8651ec88cb5333f03b8af01fee4d3cb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1319c06eac1875d14b345f09579021be</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Insulinas CANETAS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Descarte/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643613</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hiperglicemia">
  <data key="d0">Hiperglicemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hiperglicemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high levels of glucose in the blood, commonly referred to by patients as "the sugar went up." It is typically defined as blood glucose levels exceeding 100 mg/dL or surpassing the upper detection limit of glucose-monitoring devices, often indicated as "HI" on test results. This condition is frequently associated with diabetes, where it arises due to insufficient insulin production, improper insulin application, or insulin resistance, leading to inadequate glucose regulation in the body.

Common symptoms of hiperglicemia include excessive thirst, fatigue, and increased urination, which can signal the need for immediate medical attention. If left unmanaged, persistently high blood glucose levels can lead to serious complications, including diabetic ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, particularly in individuals with type 1 or type 2 diabetes. As such, hiperglicemia is considered potentially dangerous and requires prompt clinical evaluation and intervention.

Monitoring for hiperglicemia is routinely performed using capillary glucose tests, which allow for quick and accessible assessment of blood sugar levels in both clinical and home settings. These tests are essential for the ongoing management of diabetic patients and for identifying episodes of acute hyperglycemia. Multiple descriptions emphasize the link between hiperglicemia and diabetes, underscoring its role as a critical parameter in glycemic control. Overall, hiperglicemia represents a key indicator of metabolic imbalance and necessitates careful surveillance and treatment to prevent adverse health outcomes.</data>
