</node>
<node id="Glicemia Capilar">
  <data key="d0">Glicemia Capilar</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Glicemia Capilar refers to the measurement of blood glucose levels using capillary blood, typically obtained through a finger prick. This method involves collecting a small drop of blood from the fingertip, which is then analyzed using a portable glucometer equipped with disposable biosensor strips, delivering rapid results. Commonly described in clinical settings as "poking the finger" or "doing a little prick," it is a traditional and widely used approach for glucose monitoring, especially in individuals with diabetes.

The test is performed at specific times—such as during fasting and before or after meals—to monitor glucose fluctuations and support glycemic control. It plays a particularly important role in managing gestational diabetes, where regular assessment of capillary blood glucose levels in pregnant women is essential. Glicemia Capilar serves as a key component of diabetes self-monitoring, enabling both patients and healthcare providers to make informed decisions regarding treatment, medication adjustments, and lifestyle modifications.

Data from these measurements are often recorded in various formats, including paper diaries, spreadsheets, mobile applications, cloud-based software, or directly in patient medical records and monitoring charts. These records facilitate clinical follow-up and longitudinal evaluation of therapy effectiveness. Despite its accessibility, ease of use, and widespread adoption, glicemia capilar has limitations in precision and consistency when compared to continuous glucose monitoring systems. Studies indicate it is less effective in reducing hypoglycemic events and HbA1c levels, underscoring its constraints in achieving optimal long-term glycemic control. Nevertheless, it remains a fundamental tool in routine diabetes care across diverse healthcare settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1be43b1b23852278983f60d0f150b0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-022aabbd0c94aa681dcd00c49d072b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0733697cf2994b8b4b5add361db11a43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-820baa925065d77af22437295373f686&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aebc875b323050c4274610952a881b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19e4dac1289de717e1280645c07b7a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce486bcdf26ccc2a6ad63d018a57c974&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d2f09f4f3ead9f5bcbe0e4c9697887b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8b4d53f9928520ac64a197b59db6862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0778abb680b777bec45d5985b757c743</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Insulinoterapia – Diretriz da Sociedade Brasileira de Diabetes – Ed. 2024.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/cgm.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/ref.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Orientacoes_Glicemia_SBD.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/Metas no tratamento.pdf</data>
