</node>
<node id="Insulina NPH">
  <data key="d0">Insulina NPH</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulina NPH é um tipo de insulina de ação intermediária que começa a agir em 1 a 2 horas, tem pico entre 4 e 12 horas e dura até 18 a 24 horas.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina NPH é um tipo de insulina de ação intermediária fornecida pelo SUS em tubetes de 3 mL para uso em canetas reutilizáveis.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina NPH é um tipo de medicamento utilizado no tratamento do diabetes, podendo ser administrado por meio de canetas de insulina.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-022aabbd0c94aa681dcd00c49d072b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60fe44346c0b02882f7625cbf43d8ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de80027ca96b7501497bcb85811b669d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Guia-Pratico-SBD-Canetas-Reutilizaveis-de-Insulina-no-SUS.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776637998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Type 2">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Type 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Type 2 is a chronic metabolic condition characterized by the body's inability to effectively use insulin, a hormone essential for regulating blood sugar levels. This insulin resistance leads to elevated glucose levels in the bloodstream, which, if left unmanaged, can result in serious health complications. The condition is progressive in nature, and over time, individuals may develop insufficient insulin production or increasing resistance, necessitating a stepwise approach to treatment. While management typically begins with lifestyle modifications and oral medications, insulin therapy often becomes necessary when these interventions are no longer sufficient to maintain healthy blood glucose levels.

Insulin treatment is a medically appropriate and natural progression of Diabetes Type 2 management, not a reflection of personal failure or poor self-care. It is critical to emphasize this point to reduce stigma and support patient adherence to treatment plans without inducing guilt or blame. As the disease evolves, insulin therapy may be required to achieve adequate glycemic control, and its use should be normalized as a standard component of care for many individuals.

A notable physical complication associated with insulin therapy in Diabetes Type 2 is lipo-hypertrophy, which refers to the abnormal buildup of fatty tissue at insulin injection sites. This condition often results from repeated injections in the same area without proper site rotation. Studies have shown that the prevalence of lipo-hypertrophy among individuals with Diabetes Type 2 on insulin therapy ranges between 28% and 64%, underscoring the importance of proper injection techniques and regular rotation of injection sites to minimize risk. Overall, comprehensive management of Diabetes Type 2 involves not only glycemic control but also patient education on treatment practices to prevent complications and support long-term health.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 2 is a metabolic disorder characterized by insulin resistance, and individuals with this condition can also develop lipo-hypertrophy.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 2 is a condition where the body either does not produce enough insulin or cannot use it effectively, sometimes requiring insulin treatment when oral medications are insufficient.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 2 is a condition where the body either does not produce enough insulin or cannot use it effectively, sometimes requiring insulin treatment when oral medications are insufficient.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes type 2 is characterized by insulin resistance and reduced insulin production over time, impairing glucose control.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 2 is the most common form of diabetes, associated with obesity and aging, characterized by insulin resistance and partial insulin secretion deficiency, often accompanied by clinical features like acanthosis nigricans and hypertriglyceridemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03a1de93a53fbcb513268c123e2c5a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6a188926df383900f31928c8726a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f8c8b2b40633cf37993b620002b889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5847db7a558804738e20dca9fd8d746</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Complicações relacionadas ao uso da insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classificação do diabetes.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776646884</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulina Regular">
  <data key="d0">Insulina Regular</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulina Regular é um tipo de insulina que começa a agir entre 30 a 60 minutos após a aplicação, com pico entre 2 e 4 horas e duração de até 8 horas.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina Regular é um tipo de insulina de ação rápida fornecida pelo SUS em tubetes de 3 mL para uso em canetas reutilizáveis.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina Regular é um tipo de medicamento utilizado no tratamento do diabetes, podendo ser administrado por meio de canetas de insulina.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina Regular is a short-acting human insulin used in diabetes treatment, associated with higher rates of hypoglycemia compared to rapid analogs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-022aabbd0c94aa681dcd00c49d072b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60fe44346c0b02882f7625cbf43d8ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de80027ca96b7501497bcb85811b669d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a45688326f1b9dbe3fc7ae4ba51043a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Guia-Pratico-SBD-Canetas-Reutilizaveis-de-Insulina-no-SUS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Insulinoterapia – Diretriz da Sociedade Brasileira de Diabetes – Ed. 2024.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642235</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bomba de Insulina">
  <data key="d0">Bomba de Insulina</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Bomba de insulina é um dispositivo que libera insulina continuamente e pode substituir a maioria das injeções, com cateter trocado a cada 2 ou 3 dias.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-022aabbd0c94aa681dcd00c49d072b46</data>
