</node>
<node id="Air Bubbles">
  <data key="d0">Air Bubbles</data>
  <data key="d1">phenomenon</data>
  <data key="d2">Air bubbles in the syringe can affect insulin dosage accuracy and should be removed by gently tapping the syringe before injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Air bubbles in insulin vials, syringes, or pens can lead to dosing errors if not removed after vigorous shaking during homogenization.&lt;SEP&gt;Air bubbles can form in insulin vials, syringes, or pens if the solution is agitated, potentially leading to inaccurate dosing if not removed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-54575ceb6b15fc8abcc2afcec7b36792&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a81bec86298f297d9be7cc985a7eddb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dde6c55191737188788dc809811837cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776639012</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Glucose Sensor">
  <data key="d0">Glucose Sensor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose Sensor is a medical device designed for continuous glucose monitoring, serving as a modern alternative to traditional finger-prick testing by enabling frequent, minimally invasive measurement of glucose levels. Typically worn on the skin, the device consists of a small sensor inserted just beneath the skin's surface, where it measures glucose concentrations in the interstitial fluid rather than directly in the blood. This allows for ongoing, real-time tracking of glucose levels with minimal discomfort to the user.

The Glucose Sensor continuously transmits data to an external receiver, such as a dedicated reader or a smartphone application, providing users and healthcare providers with immediate access to current glucose values, directional trends (such as rising or falling levels), and alerts for hypoglycemic or hyperglycemic events. By capturing detailed patterns in glucose fluctuations—including peaks and drops throughout the day and night—it offers a comprehensive view of glycemic control that complements periodic laboratory measurements like HbA1c. Additionally, the device supports advanced diabetes management metrics, such as Time In Range, which indicates the percentage of time a person’s glucose levels remain within a target range.

Used primarily in the management of diabetes, the Glucose Sensor enhances patient autonomy and clinical decision-making through consistent, actionable data. Its ability to deliver continuous, real-time insights helps improve long-term outcomes by enabling timely interventions and more personalized treatment plans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08c7f7bb0209cabd75e27f9c8556c214&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79d0e4d0f656bd87d2d0c4657378db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12999315830b302d973dd1908eab1e12</data>
