<node id="Insulin Dose">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An insulin dose refers to the prescribed amount of insulin administered to regulate blood glucose levels in individuals with diabetes. It is a critical component of diabetes management, tailored to the specific physiological needs of each patient. The dose is typically determined based on real-time glucose readings, carbohydrate intake from meals, and other metabolic factors, allowing for adjustments to maintain optimal glycemic control.

The administration of an insulin dose may involve a single injection or require splitting into multiple injections when the prescribed amount is too high to be safely or effectively delivered in one instance. This approach ensures proper absorption and reduces the risk of localized complications. The dosing regimen is highly individualized, influenced by variables such as body weight, age, level of physical activity, and changes in health status, including illness or infection, which can alter insulin sensitivity.

Ongoing monitoring and personalized adjustments are essential, as insulin requirements can fluctuate daily. Healthcare providers often use insulin-to-carbohydrate ratios, correction factors, and glucose trend data from continuous glucose monitoring systems to refine dosing strategies. This dynamic and patient-specific nature of insulin dosing underscores its role as a cornerstone of effective diabetes care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03a1de93a53fbcb513268c123e2c5a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vascular Contact">
  <data key="d0">Vascular Contact</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vascular contact occurs when the needle touches a blood vessel during injection, potentially causing sharp pain but not serious harm.&lt;SEP&gt;Vascular contact occurs when the needle touches a blood vessel during injection, potentially causing sharp pain but not serious harm.&lt;SEP&gt;Vascular contact occurs when the needle touches a blood vessel during injection, potentially causing sharp pain but not serious harm.</data>
