  <data key="d5">1776638281</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subcutaneous Prega">
  <data key="d0">Subcutaneous Prega</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Subcutaneous prega is a technique involving a light pinch of the skin during insulin injection to ensure proper needle depth and avoid intramuscular delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Subcutaneous prega is a technique involving a light pinch of the skin during insulin injection to ensure proper needle depth and avoid intramuscular delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Subcutaneous prega is a technique involving a light pinch of the skin during insulin injection to ensure proper needle depth and avoid intramuscular delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638232</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="4 Mm Needle">
  <data key="d0">4 Mm Needle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A 4 mm needle is a short insulin injection needle recommended for children, thin adults, and pregnant women to ensure safe and effective insulin delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;A 4 mm needle is a short insulin injection needle recommended for children, thin adults, and pregnant women to ensure safe and effective insulin delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;A 4 mm needle is a short insulin injection needle recommended for children, thin adults, and pregnant women to ensure safe and effective insulin delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;The 4 mm needle is a short insulin injection needle recommended for all individuals, safe and well-tolerated, often used without a skin fold except in young children.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb18b1ccdbe7c3a6bbce149470c1ca1</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776639007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="8 Mm Needle">
  <data key="d0">8 Mm Needle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An 8 mm needle is a longer insulin injection needle that may pose a higher risk of intramuscular injection if not used with a subcutaneous prega.&lt;SEP&gt;An 8 mm needle is a longer insulin injection needle that may pose a higher risk of intramuscular injection if not used with a subcutaneous prega.&lt;SEP&gt;An 8 mm needle is a longer insulin injection needle that may pose a higher risk of intramuscular injection if not used with a subcutaneous prega.&lt;SEP&gt;The 8 mm needle is not recommended for children and adolescents due to the risk of intramuscular injection and requires a skin fold and specific insertion angles based on age and tissue availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb18b1ccdbe7c3a6bbce149470c1ca1</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776639007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="45° Injection Angle">
  <data key="d0">45° Injection Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A 45° injection angle is a technique used with shorter needles, especially during pregnancy, to ensure proper insulin delivery into subcutaneous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;A 45° injection angle is a technique used with shorter needles, especially during pregnancy, to ensure proper insulin delivery into subcutaneous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;A 45° injection angle is a technique used with shorter needles, especially during pregnancy, to ensure proper insulin delivery into subcutaneous tissue.</data>
