<node id="Gestational Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Gestational Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gestational Diabetes is a form of diabetes that develops during pregnancy, characterized by elevated blood glucose levels due to the body's inability to produce sufficient insulin to meet the increased metabolic demands of pregnancy. It is recognized as one of the main categories within the diabetes classification system and is diagnosed using specific clinical criteria, typically through glucose screening tests performed between 24 and 28 weeks of gestation. The condition poses potential risks to both the mother and fetus, including macrosomia (excessive birth weight), preterm birth, and maternal hypertension, necessitating careful monitoring and management throughout pregnancy.

Management of Gestational Diabetes primarily focuses on achieving and maintaining optimal blood glucose control. Initial treatment usually involves lifestyle modifications such as dietary changes and regular physical activity. However, when these measures are insufficient, insulin therapy becomes necessary. Multiple sources confirm that insulin is a safe and effective treatment option in this context, with clinical practice often involving rapid-acting insulin before meals and, when needed, long-acting insulin to maintain baseline glucose control. The use of insulin is well-supported in medical guidelines, reflecting a strong clinical consensus on its role in managing uncontrolled blood sugar during pregnancy.

Although Gestational Diabetes typically resolves after delivery, it carries significant long-term health implications for the mother. Women who have experienced this condition are at an increased risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life, underscoring the importance of postpartum follow-up and ongoing metabolic monitoring. Notably, Gestational Diabetes was not included in the original diabetes risk model validated by Bang et al., highlighting a gap in certain predictive frameworks despite its clinical significance as a known risk factor for future diabetes. Overall, timely diagnosis, consistent glucose monitoring, and appropriate intervention—ranging from lifestyle adjustments to insulin therapy—are essential to minimizing complications and ensuring favorable maternal and fetal outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5847db7a558804738e20dca9fd8d746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a51a13b6846af5dd45aca8b13432e7fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69bf97ae94c34166c23acdc1a684b6bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a874190e40ad535ac9b317a469571d7</data>
