<node id="Ultra-Rapid-Acting Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Ultra-Rapid-Acting Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Ultra-Rapid-Acting Insulin, such as Fiasp or inhaled insulin, begins working in 2 to 5 minutes, peaks in 30 minutes to 1 hour, and lasts up to 3 hours, used for quick blood sugar control before meals.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultra-Rapid-Acting Insulin, such as Fiasp or inhaled insulin, begins working in 2 to 5 minutes, peaks in 30 minutes to 1 hour, and lasts up to 3 hours, used for quick blood sugar control before meals.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultra-Rapid-Acting Insulin, such as Fiasp or inhaled insulin, begins working in 2 to 5 minutes, peaks in 30 minutes to 1 hour, and lasts up to 3 hours, used for quick blood sugar control before meals.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultra-rapid-acting insulin, such as Fiasp or inhaled forms, begins working in 2 to 5 minutes, peaks in 30 minutes to 1 hour, and lasts up to 3 hours; used to control mealtime glucose spikes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultra-Rapid-Acting Insulin is a fast-acting insulin used for bolus dosing with quicker onset and shorter duration than standard rapid-acting insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultra-rapid-acting insulin, such as Fiasp or inhaled forms, begins working in 2 to 5 minutes, peaks in 30 minutes to 1 hour, and lasts up to 3 hours; used to control mealtime glucose spikes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0d8750807baddcd948055c83422234</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776641986</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Long-Acting Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Long-Acting Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-Acting Insulin, such as Glargine and Detemir, begins working in 1 to 2 hours, has no peak, and lasts 20 to 24 hours, providing steady basal insulin coverage.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-Acting Insulin, such as Glargine and Detemir, begins working in 1 to 2 hours, has no peak, and lasts 20 to 24 hours, providing steady basal insulin coverage.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-Acting Insulin, such as Glargine and Detemir, begins working in 1 to 2 hours, has no peak, and lasts 20 to 24 hours, providing steady basal insulin coverage.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-acting insulin, such as Glargine and Detemir, starts working in 1 to 2 hours, has no peak, and lasts 20 to 24 hours; provides steady background insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-acting insulin, such as Glargine and Detemir, starts working in 1 to 2 hours, has no peak, and lasts 20 to 24 hours; provides steady background insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-acting insulin starts several hours after injection, has minimal peak, and lasts up to 24 hours or longer, used for basal insulin coverage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
