<node id="Basal Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Basal Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Basal Insulin refers to long-, intermediate-, or ultra-long-acting insulin formulations designed to provide a continuous, low-level supply of insulin to maintain stable blood glucose levels throughout the day and night. It functions as background insulin, regulating glucose during fasting states, between meals, and during sleep, and accounts for approximately 50% of total daily insulin requirements, particularly in individuals with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus. This steady insulin delivery mimics the natural basal secretion of insulin by the pancreas in healthy individuals. Basal insulin is commonly used in conjunction with bolus insulin, which addresses mealtime glucose spikes, to achieve comprehensive glycemic control in intensive insulin therapy regimens. It ensures consistent blood sugar management during periods of fasting and overnight, helping to prevent both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia.

The various formulations of basal insulin—ranging from long- to ultra-long-acting types—are engineered to provide prolonged and predictable insulin action with minimal peaks, supporting stable metabolic conditions over extended durations. It is delivered via subcutaneous injections or continuous infusion systems and forms the foundation of background insulin therapy. While frequently associated with Type 1 Diabetes, basal insulin is also widely used in the management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, particularly in patients requiring insulin therapy to achieve glycemic targets. Multiple descriptions emphasize its role in maintaining baseline glucose control, even in individuals who do not use intensive insulin regimens.

Basal insulin has been evaluated in clinical research, such as the MOBILE study, which assessed its use in conjunction with continuous glucose monitoring for optimizing diabetes management, particularly in type 2 diabetes. Overall, basal insulin is a critical component of insulin therapy, enabling steady glucose regulation and serving as a cornerstone in both type 1 and type 2 diabetes treatment strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1223db4d3629243420a6e867a9ba6988&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0d8750807baddcd948055c83422234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49920639f738eae3de220d132cbc081&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1abe83ba7814c7afe7a123e2f79a10a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c03aef84b96134fc1f0a2a4d8466e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f4e05859597c5f1693c2f9fe1ef0a02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df233d11f0547c43f4dc4cad909d392b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6dadde3434f68ef74ebdf23be6a24ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f12d6a3cebf5c39bed5629c359502cae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07c7e3d6615d1076ae20653358419ff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1af4e8af9934463f38c5951016d9664b</data>
