</node>
<node id="Bolus Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Bolus Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Bolus Insulin refers to rapid-, ultra-rapid-, or short-acting insulin that is administered before meals to manage blood glucose levels in response to food intake. It is a key component of intensive insulin therapy regimens, designed to mimic the body's natural insulin response during meals. The primary purpose of Bolus Insulin is to cover the carbohydrate content of meals and to correct elevated blood glucose levels when necessary.

This type of insulin is typically delivered as a bolus dose, either via insulin injection or through an insulin pump, and acts quickly to prevent postprandial hyperglycemia. By targeting glucose spikes that occur after eating, Bolus Insulin helps maintain overall glycemic control in individuals with diabetes, particularly those with type 1 diabetes or insulin-requiring type 2 diabetes. Its use is often coordinated with meal timing and carbohydrate counting to optimize effectiveness and minimize the risk of hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bolus insulin is delivered in 2-unit increments via patch devices or in conjunction with basal insulin injections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e6ef38db0b62d161b13473af47d738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1223db4d3629243420a6e867a9ba6988&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0d8750807baddcd948055c83422234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7fb8feaa2febe2b5f8525b93f3080f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df233d11f0547c43f4dc4cad909d392b</data>
