<node id="Dysglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Dysglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dysglycemia refers to abnormal blood glucose levels characterized by instability, frequent oscillations, or fluctuations in blood sugar. It encompasses both hyperglycemia (elevated glucose levels) and hypoglycemia (low glucose levels), representing a broad spectrum of glucose dysregulation that includes conditions such as prediabetes and diabetes. This metabolic state is not only a marker of existing glucose disorders but also serves as an indicator of the near-term risk of developing clinical diabetes, making it a valuable focus in early detection and preventive care.

The condition is associated with significant physiological consequences, including internal inflammation and vascular damage, which contribute to long-term complications, particularly in individuals with sustained glucose instability. Dysglycemia can be identified through diagnostic methods such as the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), where a one-hour post-load glucose measurement is predictive of its presence. This early identification is especially relevant in clinical settings like dentistry, where dysglycemia has been found to be prevalent among a significant portion of dental patients over the age of 30, highlighting its broader public health implications. Overall, dysglycemia plays a critical role in diabetes diagnosis and metabolic health assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Dysglycemia refers to abnormal blood glucose levels, including both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia, often preceding diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Dysglycemia is a condition of abnormal blood glucose levels predicted by one-hour post-load glucose during OGTT.&lt;SEP&gt;Dysglycemia refers to abnormal blood glucose levels, including both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia, often preceding diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Dysglycemia is a condition of abnormal blood glucose levels predicted by one-hour post-load glucose during OGTT.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620e2d1316d6093170f1c9991caabc7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb9c311fc732ddd262c56ddc696da954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fff9929a18d525e3805a8ed08356d41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f017257ae0e7fcc5a0a1be0ddc435fe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2977bb9a41689e74de2c536343624744</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classificação do diabetes.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688979</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inflammation">
  <data key="d0">Inflammation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inflammation is an internal bodily response triggered by dysglycemia that damages tissues and blood vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammation is an internal bodily response triggered by dysglycemia, contributing to tissue and organ damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammation is a contributing factor to insulin secretory defects in type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammation is a contributing factor to insulin secretory defects in type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647362</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vascular Damage">
  <data key="d0">Vascular Damage</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Vascular damage refers to injury to blood vessels caused by prolonged and fluctuating high blood sugar levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Vascular damage refers to harm to blood vessels caused by prolonged or fluctuating high blood sugar levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776644585</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metabolic Memory">
  <data key="d0">Metabolic Memory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolic memory is the phenomenon where the body retains the negative effects of past high blood sugar levels, even after glucose control improves.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic memory is the phenomenon where the body retains negative effects of past high blood sugar levels, even after glucose control improves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776644585</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fasting Blood Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Fasting Blood Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Fasting blood glucose is a measurement of blood sugar levels before eating, used to assess diabetes control.&lt;SEP&gt;Fasting blood glucose refers to blood sugar levels measured after an overnight fast, with general targets between 80 and 130 mg/dL for adults and 70 to 130 mg/dL for children and adolescents.&lt;SEP&gt;Fasting blood glucose should be measured four times daily in gestational diabetes, with target levels below 95 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Fasting blood glucose is a measurement of blood sugar levels before eating, used to assess diabetes control.&lt;SEP&gt;Fasting blood glucose is a measurement of blood sugar levels after an overnight fast, used to assess glycemic control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ad6cd6b0bad77397aa6685ccb2cfaef</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/Metas no tratamento.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689299</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Postprandial Blood Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Postprandial Blood Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Postprandial blood glucose refers to blood sugar levels after meals, monitored to evaluate treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Postprandial blood glucose measures sugar levels two hours after meals, with a common target of less than 180 mg/dL for most individuals with diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Postprandial blood glucose should be measured 1 hour after meals, with target levels below 140 mg/dL, or 2 hours after meals below 120 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Postprandial blood glucose refers to blood sugar levels measured after meals, used to evaluate glucose response to food.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776644586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
