<node id="Insulin Application Rotation">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Application Rotation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin application rotation is the practice of changing injection sites to prevent tissue damage and lipo-hypertrophy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638261</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Needle Use">
  <data key="d0">New Needle Use</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">New needle use is the recommended practice of using a fresh needle for every insulin injection to reduce complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638262</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Discovery">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Discovery</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The discovery of insulin occurred in 1921, marking a major advancement in diabetes treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The insulin discovery refers to the series of experiments in 1921–1922 that led to the first successful use of insulin to treat diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The discovery of insulin in 1921 marked a breakthrough in diabetes treatment, initially tested in dogs before human use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776644586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Vial Storage">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Vial Storage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin vial storage refers to the proper handling of opened and unopened insulin vials, with opened vials lasting 30 to 45 days and stored in a plastic container away from direct heat or light.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afd148f1edc7929cecea8e4d981ed04e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas FRASCOS.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638262</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blood Ketone Measurement">
  <data key="d0">Blood Ketone Measurement</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood ketone measurement is a faster and more reliable method compared to urine testing for monitoring ketone levels in people with diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638265</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Urine Ketone Measurement">
  <data key="d0">Urine Ketone Measurement</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Urine ketone measurement is a less reliable and slower method for detecting ketone levels compared to blood testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638266</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ketone Testing">
  <data key="d0">Ketone Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Ketone testing is recommended under specific conditions such as high blood glucose, illness, or insulin pump failure to prevent complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638266</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blood Glucose Level">
  <data key="d0">Blood Glucose Level</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood glucose level is a key health metric monitored in diabetes management, with specific thresholds indicating risk, such as above 250 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Glucose Level is a measurable physiological parameter, with normal fasting values ranging from 70 to 100 mg/dL and pre-meal values from 90 to 150 mg/dL in certain clinical contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0d8750807baddcd948055c83422234</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776641779</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fever">
  <data key="d0">Fever</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fever is one of the symptoms that triggers the need for ketone testing in individuals with diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fever increases insulin absorption rate due to elevated metabolic activity and blood flow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dde6c55191737188788dc809811837cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776639037</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vomiting">
  <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Vomiting is a condition that indicates the necessity of performing ketone testing to avoid diabetic complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638266</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abdominal Pain">
  <data key="d0">Abdominal Pain</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Abdominal pain is a symptom that warrants ketone testing in diabetic patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638267</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infection">
  <data key="d0">Infection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Signs of infection are a trigger for ketone testing in people with diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Infection is a stressor that can trigger DKA in individuals with type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a51a13b6846af5dd45aca8b13432e7fc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647317</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adolescents With Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Adolescents With Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Adolescents with diabetes are a high-risk group recommended for regular ketone testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638267</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blood Ketone Test Strips">
  <data key="d0">Blood Ketone Test Strips</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood ketone test strips are difficult to obtain in the public health network in Brazil.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638267</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standardized Guidance">
  <data key="d0">Standardized Guidance</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Standardized guidance on when and how to use ketone testing is currently lacking in Brazil.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638268</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pregnancy is a physiological state that significantly impacts glucose metabolism and the management of diabetes in women, particularly those with pre-existing conditions such as type 1 or type 2 diabetes. Strict glycemic control is essential during pregnancy to minimize risks to both the mother and the developing fetus, as uncontrolled blood glucose levels can lead to adverse outcomes including congenital anomalies, preterm birth, and macrosomia. For women with type 1 diabetes who experience poor glycemic control or frequent episodes of hypoglycemia, continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (CSII), commonly known as insulin pump therapy, is considered a beneficial and effective treatment strategy to optimize insulin delivery and maintain stable glucose levels throughout pregnancy.

In addition to the challenges of insulin management, pregnancy alters hematological parameters, which in turn affects the accuracy of standard diabetes monitoring tools. Notably, hemoglobin glycation processes are modified during pregnancy, particularly in the second and third trimesters, leading to reduced reliability of glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c or A1C) as a diagnostic and monitoring tool. These changes are attributed to altered red blood cell dynamics, including shifts in lifespan and turnover, which compromise the ability of A1C to reflect true average blood glucose levels. As a result, alternative monitoring methods such as self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) or continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) are recommended to ensure accurate assessment and effective diabetes management during this critical period.&lt;SEP&gt;Pregnancy is a physiological state that affects red blood cell turnover and glycemic targets, with specific diabetes management guidelines addressed separately.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed9228bfdd30fe45392a8a9fb8d106b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182332eb7daaa8deed5e90a79db308e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a79d0e4d0f656bd87d2d0c4657378db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20b3e88dda40e2d303af38aea0d19b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146b6bf0e9a99df13de25f5e1318178a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f7252ca9147660b04b7329edbe843f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/Metas no tratamento.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689264</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Women With Preexisting Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Women With Preexisting Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Women with preexisting diabetes (type 1 or 2) must carefully plan pregnancy due to risks of high glucose affecting fetal development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638268</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Glucose During Early Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">High Glucose During Early Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">High glucose levels in early pregnancy increase the risk of fetal malformation in women with preexisting diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638268</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Glycemic Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">Glycemic Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Glycemic monitoring involves measuring blood glucose 7 times daily during pregnancy for women with preexisting diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycemic Monitoring is the process of tracking blood glucose levels to inform insulin dosing and diabetes management decisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0d8750807baddcd948055c83422234</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/sici.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776641778</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trimester">
  <data key="d0">Trimester</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">HbA1c should be measured every trimester during pregnancy, with specific target values for each stage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638268</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Glucose Monitor">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Glucose Monitor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuous glucose monitors (CGM) help pregnant women with diabetes spend more time in target glucose range and reduce hypoglycemia and neonatal complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638268</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neonatal ICU">
  <data key="d0">Neonatal ICU</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Babies of mothers with diabetes have lower chances of requiring neonatal ICU if mothers use continuous glucose monitoring during pregnancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638268</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Medical Association">
  <data key="d0">Medical Association</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Medical associations such as ACOG, ADA, CDA, IDF, and NICE provide recommended glycemic targets during pregnancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776638269</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ACOG">
  <data key="d0">ACOG</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">ACOG is a medical association in the US that recommends fasting glucose below 90 mg/dL, &lt;140 mg/dL at 1h post-meal, and &lt;120 mg/dL at 2h post-meal.&lt;SEP&gt;ACOG is the American Congress of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, a scientific society providing guidelines on glycemic targets in pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG) recommends thresholds for the 1-h 50-g GLT in GDM screening.&lt;SEP&gt;The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG) supports the two-step approach for GDM diagnosis and allows for diagnosis with one elevated value on the 3-hour OGTT.&lt;SEP&gt;ACOG is a medical association in the US that provides recommended blood glucose targets for pregnant women with diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aebc875b323050c4274610952a881b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7869f89a9c58ace31efe679a36aab7cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d936160aac8a6b8ba40b196280422fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dce7a759a9de18a4d1bcc77a071fbe2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/ref.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776648239</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ADA">
  <data key="d0">ADA</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The American Diabetes Association (ADA) is a prominent US-based medical and scientific organization dedicated to diabetes care, research, and advocacy. It establishes comprehensive clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis, screening, and management of diabetes across diverse populations, including pediatric patients and pregnant individuals. The ADA recommends specific glycemic targets to optimize health outcomes, such as fasting glucose levels below 95 mg/dL, postprandial glucose levels under 140 mg/dL at one hour and less than 120 mg/dL at two hours after meals, and an HbA1c target of ≤7.5% for children with diabetes.

In pediatric care, the ADA supports the use of continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion systems for young children with diabetes to improve glycemic control and treatment adherence. It also advocates for the use of A1C as a diagnostic and monitoring tool in children, despite acknowledged limitations in pediatric data, reflecting its commitment to evidence-based practices while recognizing areas needing further research. For gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), the ADA recommends adopting the diagnostic criteria established by the International Association of the Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups (IADPSG), aiming to enhance maternal and fetal health outcomes through early detection and intervention.

Overall, the ADA plays a central role in shaping diabetes care standards, emphasizing individualized treatment, scientific rigor, and the integration of emerging technologies and criteria to improve patient outcomes across the lifespan.&lt;SEP&gt;ADA is a US-based medical organization that sets guidelines for diabetes management, including glycemic targets during pregnancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-768c26e20aaed169fbf07de7ca22c08d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0733697cf2994b8b4b5add361db11a43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aebc875b323050c4274610952a881b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-357f19fa58c4dd99dcd7b47a984b4383&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb9c311fc732ddd262c56ddc696da954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dce7a759a9de18a4d1bcc77a071fbe2</data>
