<node id="Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII), commonly known as insulin pump therapy, is a method of insulin delivery that uses a pump to administer insulin subcutaneously in a continuous manner. The system delivers insulin in two primary forms: basal rates, which provide a steady background infusion to maintain glucose control between meals and overnight, and bolus doses, which are manually administered to cover carbohydrate intake at meals or to correct high blood glucose levels. This therapeutic approach requires careful calculation of individualized parameters such as total daily insulin dose, insulin sensitivity factor, and insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio to optimize glycemic management.

CSII is widely used in the treatment of type 1 diabetes and is particularly recommended for specific patient populations, including children, adolescents, pregnant women, and very young children with type 1 diabetes. It has also been identified as a beneficial option for diabetic patients who experience hypoglycemic unawareness, as the precision and flexibility of insulin delivery may reduce the risk of severe hypoglycemic episodes. Clinical guidelines, including a position statement by the Lawson-Wilkins Drug and Therapeutics Committee, have addressed the use of CSII in pediatric populations, underscoring its role in specialized diabetes care.

Compared to Multiple Daily Injections (MDI), CSII is generally more expensive due to the cost of the pump device and associated supplies. However, studies suggest that it may offer improved glycemic control, greater treatment flexibility, and enhanced quality of life for certain individuals. As the technical term for insulin pump therapy, CSII represents a significant advancement in diabetes management, enabling more physiologic insulin replacement and tailored treatment regimens for diverse patient needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion is an intensive insulin delivery method using a pump to provide steady and adjustable insulin supply for type 1 diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (CSII), commonly delivered via insulin pumps, is a method of insulin delivery for diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion is an insulin delivery method using a pump to provide a steady flow of insulin under the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion is the technical term for insulin delivery via an insulin pump, requiring ongoing access across healthcare payers.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion is a method of insulin delivery compared to multiple daily injections in various clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion is an insulin delivery method using a pump to administer insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eed9228bfdd30fe45392a8a9fb8d106b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182332eb7daaa8deed5e90a79db308e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddf3203a55511f7874bc210d61b3ccfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45bbbee62d25d825935842af1930bb44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7be6907597f74116387925485497fbd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-768c26e20aaed169fbf07de7ca22c08d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8d6904368d45b0f9532b36061a00e7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bddc99b2e70c378921ff59ddb3b954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49920639f738eae3de220d132cbc081&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90452c5625b363eb503added0caf901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0cc6a5cedffa24f9a3134770cf1526b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36191c270d1cc8ff8c1fc610d7a533b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f12d6a3cebf5c39bed5629c359502cae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdbe9f47931df265e815faee2c67f81b</data>
