<node id="N Engl J Med">
  <data key="d0">N Engl J Med</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The New England Journal of Medicine (N Engl J Med) is a prestigious medical journal renowned for publishing high-impact, peer-reviewed clinical research that has significantly influenced medical practice and policy. It has served as a key platform for landmark studies in diabetes care, covering both type 1 and type 2 diabetes, as well as gestational diabetes. The journal has published pivotal trials on advanced treatment modalities for type 1 diabetes, including sensor-augmented insulin-pump therapy and continuous glucose monitoring combined with intensive treatment regimens, with notable studies appearing as early as 2008. These studies have demonstrated improvements in glycemic control and patient outcomes, reinforcing the role of technology and rigorous management in diabetes care.

N Engl J Med has also featured groundbreaking research on the genetic basis of diabetes, including studies on patients with Kir6.2 mutations who were successfully transitioned from insulin therapy to sulfonylureas, highlighting the clinical implications of precision medicine. In the realm of type 2 diabetes prevention, the journal published influential findings from the Diabetes Prevention Program Research Group and the Finnish Diabetes Prevention Study Group, which demonstrated that lifestyle interventions and metformin could significantly reduce the incidence of the disease. These studies have informed public health strategies worldwide and underscored the importance of early intervention.

Additionally, the journal has advanced understanding in immunomodulatory treatments for diabetes, publishing clinical trial results on teplizumab and its effects on delaying the onset of type 1 diabetes, as measured by markers such as glycated hemoglobin. It has also contributed to obstetric medicine by publishing randomized trials on the management of mild gestational diabetes, showing that treatment can improve pregnancy outcomes. Through its consistent publication of rigorous, practice-changing research across multiple domains of diabetes and beyond, the New England Journal of Medicine remains a cornerstone of evidence-based medicine.&lt;SEP&gt;N Engl J Med is a medical journal that published research on glycated hemoglobin and cardiovascular risk.&lt;SEP&gt;N Engl J Med is a medical journal that published research on glycated hemoglobin and cardiovascular risk.&lt;SEP&gt;N Engl J Med is a medical journal that published several studies on diabetes and glucose control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ddf3203a55511f7874bc210d61b3ccfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6764f5f0af1c9855a1901ea1bef131d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f4e05859597c5f1693c2f9fe1ef0a02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-755e9677a7018e4cc382b7d903590786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad3acc8463b5ff47461b1c16ae2559cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45a8ca206e61b47964f899c929fbafb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05070e0755e36e3375d401400cf75fce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f76e40d0d9b4211d8c61a53ea298d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3d27a8dbb6882ee6a732de5ac202d64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f017257ae0e7fcc5a0a1be0ddc435fe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df3628fc721a312bfbaace1de3a4d877</data>
