<node id="JAMA">
  <data key="d0">JAMA</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">JAMA is a major peer-reviewed medical journal that has published extensive research on diabetes and related health conditions. The journal has featured clinical studies on various aspects of diabetes management, including the safety and efficacy of insulin pump therapy, continuous glucose monitoring in adolescents and young adults with type 1 diabetes, and the performance of hybrid closed-loop insulin delivery systems. These publications highlight JAMA's role in advancing clinical understanding of technological interventions for diabetes care.

In addition to treatment technologies, JAMA has published significant research on the epidemiology and risk factors associated with diabetes. This includes studies on the prevalence and trends of diabetes among adults and youth in the United States, as well as investigations into early markers of diabetes development, such as seroconversion to multiple islet autoantibodies in children and their association with future diabetes risk. The journal has also reported on diabetes screening protocols and the presence of autoantibodies, with key studies appearing in 2014 and 2020.

JAMA has further contributed to understanding the biological and social determinants of diabetes-related metrics. For instance, it published a study examining the association between sickle cell trait and hemoglobin A1c levels in African Americans, which has implications for the accuracy of diabetes diagnosis in this population. Additionally, the journal has explored the long-term impacts of gestational diabetes, reporting on its association with maternal metabolism and increased childhood adiposity. Collectively, these publications underscore JAMA's prominence in disseminating high-impact research across the spectrum of diabetes prevention, diagnosis, and public health implications.</data>
