<node id="Diabetologia">
  <data key="d0">Diabetologia</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetologia is a scientific journal dedicated to publishing high-quality research on diabetes, metabolic disorders, and related conditions, serving as a leading platform for advancing the understanding, diagnosis, and treatment of diabetes. It emphasizes clinical and translational research, featuring a broad range of study types such as randomized controlled trials, systematic reviews, meta-analyses, and genetic investigations. The journal covers diverse aspects of diabetology, including pathophysiology, genetics, and the clinical management of both type 1 and type 2 diabetes, reflecting its comprehensive and multidisciplinary scope.

Diabetologia has published influential research on various treatment modalities, including comparative studies of insulin analogues such as detemir and glargine, with a notable 2008 study (Volume 51, pages 408–416) contributing to the clinical understanding of basal insulin options. It has also evaluated insulin delivery methods, comparing pump-based administration to injections, and has featured meta-analyses on the effectiveness of continuous glucose monitoring in type 1 diabetes, particularly in publications from 2012 and 2019. These contributions highlight the journal’s engagement with evolving technologies and therapeutic strategies in diabetes care.

The journal has significantly advanced knowledge on the genetic and phenotypic heterogeneity of diabetes. It has published studies on de novo mutations in genes associated with maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) and has contributed to the development of prediction models for MODY and type 1 diabetes. Diabetologia also published research on type 1 diabetes occurring after age 30 and on the reclassification of diabetes subtypes, reflecting its interest in refining diagnostic criteria. A key 2015 study published in the journal identified one-hour plasma glucose levels as a predictor of type 2 diabetes, reinforcing its role in identifying early metabolic markers. A 2020 publication further addressed diabetes heterogeneity, underscoring the journal’s ongoing commitment to exploring the diverse mechanisms and subtypes of the disease. Collectively, Diabetologia remains a pivotal forum for innovative, high-impact research in the field of diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia is a scientific journal publishing research on diabetes and related metabolic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia is a leading diabetes research journal that published a 2015 study on one-hour plasma glucose as a predictor of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia is a journal that published research on 1-hour plasma glucose as a predictor of type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetologia is a scientific journal that published findings on intensive glucose control and macrovascular outcomes in type 2 diabetes.</data>
