</node>
<node id="Hyperglycaemia">
  <data key="d0">Hyperglycaemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperglycaemia refers to high blood sugar levels and may require treatment with insulin, especially in diabetic patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperglycaemia refers to high blood glucose levels, which can result from ineffective insulin production or use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642504</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Vials">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Vials</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin vials are containers used for storing insulin, typically used with syringes for injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642504</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypoglycaemia">
  <data key="d0">Hypoglycaemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypoglycaemia is a side effect of insulin therapy characterized by low blood sugar, with symptoms including shakiness, sweating, confusion, and potential loss of consciousness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642504</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Site Reactions">
  <data key="d0">Injection Site Reactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection site reactions include redness, swelling, or itching at the location where insulin is administered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642504</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Allergic Reactions">
  <data key="d0">Allergic Reactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Allergic reactions to insulin are rare but possible, with symptoms such as rash, difficulty breathing, or swelling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642505</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin and How to Use It Safely">
  <data key="d0">Insulin and How to Use It Safely</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin and How to Use It Safely is a free online course by the International Diabetes Federation that explains insulin and its safe use for people with type 1, type 2, and gestational diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642505</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="It's About Timing and Targeting">
  <data key="d0">It's About Timing and Targeting</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">It's About Timing and Targeting is a webinar for healthcare professionals offering guidance on implementing once-weekly basal insulin for people with type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dd0ac30945c224efc81753de13bdebf</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642505</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frederick Banting">
  <data key="d0">Frederick Banting</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Frederick Banting was a researcher who conducted early experiments leading to the discovery of insulin in Toronto in 1921.&lt;SEP&gt;Frederick Banting is a researcher at the University of Toronto who conducts experiments on dogs to develop insulin, beginning in May 1921.&lt;SEP&gt;Frederick Banting is a researcher at the University of Toronto who conducts experiments on dogs to develop insulin, beginning in May 1921.&lt;SEP&gt;Frederick Banting is a researcher at the University of Toronto who conducts experiments on dogs to develop insulin, beginning in May 1921.&lt;SEP&gt;Frederick Banting is a researcher at the University of Toronto who conducts experiments on dogs to develop insulin, beginning in May 1921.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb1d6cf513719b7859327bdb2f78fee4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Charles Best">
  <data key="d0">Charles Best</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Charles Best was a researcher who worked with Frederick Banting on experiments involving insulin extraction and its effects on diabetic dogs.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles Best is a fourth-year student in Physiology and Biochemistry at the University of Toronto who assists Frederick Banting in testing blood sugar, nitrogen, and sugar levels in urine during their research.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles Best is a fourth-year student in Physiology and Biochemistry at the University of Toronto who assists Frederick Banting in testing blood sugar, nitrogen, and sugar levels in urine during their research.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles Best is a fourth-year student in Physiology and Biochemistry at the University of Toronto who assists Frederick Banting in testing blood sugar, nitrogen, and sugar levels in urine during their research.&lt;SEP&gt;Charles Best is a fourth-year student in Physiology and Biochemistry at the University of Toronto who assists Frederick Banting in testing blood sugar, nitrogen, and sugar levels in urine during their research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb1d6cf513719b7859327bdb2f78fee4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marjorie">
  <data key="d0">Marjorie</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Marjorie was a dog used in insulin experiments by the Toronto research team and survived for 70 days with injections of pancreatic extract.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjorie is the name of Dog #33, a diabetic dog used in the longevity experiment after its pancreas was removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjorie is the name of Dog #33, a diabetic dog used in the longevity experiment after its pancreas was removed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643972</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Toronto">
  <data key="d0">Toronto</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Toronto is a city in Canada where the initial experiments on insulin were conducted in 1921.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto is the city where the University of Toronto and its associated research facilities are located.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto is the location of the University of Toronto and the site of early insulin research, clinical testing, and Connaught Laboratories.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto is the city where the University of Toronto and its associated research facilities are located.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto is the location of the University of Toronto and the site of early insulin research, clinical testing, and Connaught Laboratories.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto is the city where the University of Toronto and its associated research facilities are located.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto is the location of the University of Toronto and the site of early insulin research, clinical testing, and Connaught Laboratories.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Animal Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Animal Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Animal insulin is a type of insulin extracted from the pancreases of beef and pork, used historically and still by some individuals today.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biosynthetic Human Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Biosynthetic Human Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Biosynthetic human insulin (BHI) is a laboratory-produced insulin that mimics naturally produced human insulin, developed in the 1980s.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Analogues">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Analogues</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin analogues are genetically engineered synthetic insulins designed to have modified action profiles for better glucose management.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin analogues are modern therapeutic agents used in diabetes treatment that may reduce the risk of hypoglycemia compared to traditional insulin therapies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02fb9dc2bae2939b212aa5df09194ee4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/Metas no tratamento.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biosimilar Insulins">
  <data key="d0">Biosimilar Insulins</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Biosimilar insulins (BI) are insulin products developed to be similar to existing insulins but with slight molecular differences due to different production processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Basal Insulins">
  <data key="d0">Basal Insulins</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Basal insulins, also known as long-acting or background insulins, are taken once or twice daily to maintain stable blood glucose levels between meals and overnight.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bolus Insulins">
  <data key="d0">Bolus Insulins</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Bolus insulins are rapid-acting insulins taken before meals to manage the spike in blood glucose after eating.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642508</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mixed Insulins">
  <data key="d0">Mixed Insulins</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Mixed insulins are premixed formulations combining slow-acting and rapid-acting insulins, used before meals without requiring separate basal insulin injections.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixed insulins are premixed combinations of short-acting or rapid-acting insulin with intermediate-acting insulin, appearing cloudy and used without separate basal insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642509</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1922 Insulin Injection">
  <data key="d0">1922 Insulin Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The first successful injection of insulin into a person living with diabetes occurred on 23 January 1922, marking a pivotal moment in medical history.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mealtime Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Mealtime Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealtime insulin is a type of insulin taken before meals to manage blood glucose levels, typically involving short-acting or rapid-acting insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealtime insulin is a type of insulin taken before meals to manage postprandial glucose spikes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0a49a3e1aba9780862b94a266490691</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643606</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Short-Acting Insulin">
  <data key="d0">Short-Acting Insulin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Short-acting insulin begins working within 30 minutes, peaks at 2–3 hours, and lasts 3–6 hours, used to control blood glucose around meals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sliding Scale Therapy">
  <data key="d0">Sliding Scale Therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Sliding scale therapy adjusts pre-meal or nighttime insulin doses based on predefined blood glucose ranges, requiring consistent food intake and activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642512</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Infusion">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Infusion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin infusion involves delivering insulin intravenously, typically used in hospital settings for diabetic ketoacidosis or during surgery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642512</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Individual Lifestyle">
  <data key="d0">Individual Lifestyle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Individual lifestyle factors such as work schedule, meal patterns, and physical activity influence the choice of insulin regimen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642513</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Regimen">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Regimen</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An insulin regimen refers to the structured plan for insulin administration, which varies based on diabetes type and individual needs, including timing, dosage, and delivery method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776642513</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Production">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Production</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0522904b9c236fceb86e75d6da872d4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1961d5ebd8cd94a8e08ca46c59210d51</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin Production refers to the large-scale development and manufacturing process of insulin for medical use, a breakthrough that emerged in the early 1920s through collaborative scientific efforts. The process was jointly advanced by researchers at the University of Toronto and the pharmaceutical company Eli Lilly, marking a pivotal moment in the treatment of diabetes. This partnership enabled the transition of insulin from a laboratory discovery to a clinically available therapy, setting the foundation for modern biopharmaceutical production.

A key figure in the early stages of insulin production was Charles Best, who, alongside Frederick Banting and others at Connaught Laboratories—affiliated with the University of Toronto—played a central role in scaling up the manufacturing process. Connaught Laboratories managed the large-scale production of insulin under Best’s oversight, ensuring its availability for medical use in the critical early years following its discovery. The initial method involved extracting insulin from the pancreases of animals, primarily beef and pork, which became the first form of insulin used in human treatments.

Despite the scientific success, insulin production in 1922 faced significant practical challenges, including difficulties in securing sufficient raw materials—animal pancreases—and maintaining consistent yields during extraction. These logistical hurdles underscored the complexity of scaling a biologically derived therapeutic and highlighted the need for standardized, reliable manufacturing protocols. The repeated emphasis on collaboration between the University of Toronto and Eli Lilly, as well as Best’s leadership at Connaught Laboratories, reflects the multifaceted nature of early insulin production, combining academic research, industrial capacity, and medical innovation to meet urgent public health needs.</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/Understanding insulin - International Diabetes Federation.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hospital Stay">
  <data key="d0">Hospital Stay</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2841f77ed9254c0e5372f3c45f6d3da2</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin infusion is used during hospital stays for diabetes management, particularly during surgery or acute conditions.</data>
