  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Connaught">
  <data key="d0">Connaught</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Connaught, formally known as Connaught Laboratories, is a research and development institution affiliated with the University of Toronto that played a foundational role in the development, standardization, and large-scale production of insulin for public use. Established with a mission rooted in public health, Connaught entered into a formal agreement with the pioneering researchers Frederick Banting, Charles Best, James Collip, and John Macleod to advance their discovery of insulin into a safe, effective, and widely accessible treatment for diabetes. Under the leadership of Dr. J.G. FitzGerald, who served as director, the institution spearheaded scientific and operational efforts to refine insulin production methods, ensuring consistency and reliability in therapeutic applications.

With key support from Robert D. Defries, Connaught Laboratories developed a standardized manufacturing process that enabled mass production of insulin, aligning with its nonprofit, publicly driven mandate. To meet escalating global demand, the institution collaborated with pharmaceutical partners, most notably Eli Lilly, to establish scalable and dependable supplies of insulin for diabetic patients worldwide. This partnership was instrumental in transforming a laboratory breakthrough into a life-saving medical therapy available on an international scale.

Through its innovation, institutional collaboration, and commitment to equitable access, Connaught Laboratories became a cornerstone in the early history of insulin therapy. Its work not only ensured the clinical viability of insulin but also set a precedent for publicly funded, nonprofit biomedical research and development in the 20th century, leaving a lasting legacy in the field of medical science and public health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1961d5ebd8cd94a8e08ca46c59210d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
