<node id="Toronto General Hospital">
  <data key="d0">Toronto General Hospital</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Toronto General Hospital is a pivotal medical institution in the history of diabetes treatment, most notably recognized as the site where the first human administration of insulin occurred. This landmark event marked a transformative moment in medical science, providing a life-saving therapy for patients suffering from diabetes. The hospital served as a central location for the early clinical application of insulin, developed through collaborative research efforts in the early 1920s.

At Toronto General Hospital, Dr. James Collip worked in a laboratory to purify the pancreatic extract that would become insulin, refining the substance to make it safe and effective for human use. His contributions were critical to the success of the treatment trials conducted at the hospital. Among the early recipients of insulin therapy was Charlotte Clarke, who was admitted to the facility and considered for one of the initial treatments, highlighting the hospital’s role in patient care during this experimental phase.

By the end of December 1922, Toronto General Hospital had successfully treated 50 diabetic patients with insulin, demonstrating the therapy’s remarkable efficacy. Notably, ten of these patients were revived from diabetic comas, underscoring the dramatic impact of the treatment. These achievements solidified the hospital’s legacy as a cornerstone in the development and implementation of insulin therapy, contributing significantly to the global advancement of endocrinology and diabetes care.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto General Hospital is the location where Collip worked on purifying the pancreatic extract in a laboratory.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto General Hospital is the medical facility where Charlotte Clarke was admitted and where early insulin treatments were considered.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto General Hospital is where the first human administration of insulin took place.&lt;SEP&gt;Toronto General Hospital (TGH) is where 50 diabetic cases were successfully treated with insulin by the end of December 1922, including ten patients revived from comas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3b5ce540c7784f49191a479629af78e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
