<node id="Defining Moments Canada">
  <data key="d0">Defining Moments Canada</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Defining Moments Canada is an organization dedicated to documenting, preserving, and sharing pivotal events in Canadian history, with a strong emphasis on major achievements in Canadian science. Central to its mission is the comprehensive chronicling of the discovery and development of insulin, one of Canada’s most significant scientific and medical breakthroughs. The organization serves as the steward of extensive digital resources that detail this milestone, ensuring the historical, scientific, and social dimensions of insulin’s legacy are accessible to the public, educators, and students. As the custodian of the online record on insulin’s history, Defining Moments Canada plays a critical role in maintaining and disseminating accurate, engaging, and educational content about this transformative advancement in diabetes treatment.

A cornerstone of the organization’s work is the Insulin100 project, launched to commemorate the centenary of insulin’s discovery. Through this initiative, Defining Moments Canada hosts the Insulin100 timeline, an interactive digital platform that traces key milestones in insulin’s development, highlights the contributions of pioneering scientists such as Frederick Banting and Charles Best, and examines the global impact of insulin on public health. This project combines rigorous historical research with innovative digital storytelling to foster national reflection, critical thinking, and a deeper understanding of Canada’s scientific heritage. The organization leverages this and similar initiatives to make historical events both accessible and relevant to contemporary audiences.

Beyond the Insulin100 project, Defining Moments Canada produces a wide range of curriculum-aligned educational content on other defining moments in Canadian history. By integrating accurate historical scholarship with engaging narrative formats, the organization supports public education and promotes awareness of Canada’s national heritage. Its resources are designed to be inclusive and widely accessible, ensuring that pivotal events in Canadian history are not only preserved but also meaningfully connected to modern-day learning and civic identity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-030fb051231df38c20ff2e1c1e73153d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1961d5ebd8cd94a8e08ca46c59210d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-572090790565a204f99a705ed03cf526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0eaeeaa0051cb78248f45d6af3009e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-039feb72dc6f2619a95a3b925679cc65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Best">
  <data key="d0">Best</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Best is a researcher who played a central role in the development and testing of pancreatic extracts for the treatment of diabetes during the early 1920s, working closely with Frederick Banting. His collaborative research focused on extracting substances from animal pancreases and evaluating their effectiveness in treating diabetes, particularly through experiments conducted on diabetic dogs. These efforts were critical in the refinement of "Isletin," the early name for what would later be known as insulin. Best actively participated in key experimental phases, including testing the pancreatic extract on himself to assess its safety and physiological effects, demonstrating both scientific dedication and personal risk in advancing the research.

Best was present during pivotal moments leading up to the first human trial of insulin, contributing significantly to the scientific foundation that enabled its clinical application. Following the initial breakthroughs, he was entrusted with overseeing insulin development, purification, and large-scale production at the Connaught Laboratories, a role that highlighted his instrumental position in transforming laboratory discoveries into a practical and life-saving medical treatment. In recognition of his contributions, Best co-held the U.S. patent application for insulin with James Collip, affirming his key involvement in the invention and refinement of the hormone’s extraction process. Through his extensive collaboration with Banting and his leadership in insulin production, Best helped lay the groundwork for one of the most transformative medical advancements of the 20th century.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c04d944f188525956166c3081e7e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643968</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Banting">
  <data key="d0">Banting</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Banting was a Canadian physician and medical researcher best known for his pivotal role in the discovery and development of insulin, a groundbreaking treatment for diabetes. Working at the University of Toronto, he secured a lecturer position that enabled him to pursue research on pancreatic extracts, collaborating closely with Charles Best. Together, they investigated the substance responsible for blood sugar regulation, initially referring to their experimental extract as "Isletin." Their work culminated in a major breakthrough when they successfully treated diabetic dogs with the extract, most notably Dog #92, which showed dramatic recovery and provided critical evidence of the therapy’s efficacy.

Banting’s contributions extended from laboratory research to direct clinical application. He was one of the lead researchers in the first human trials of insulin, actively participating in patient selection and treatment. He administered insulin to several early patients, including Elizabeth Hughes, daughter of U.S. Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes, and closely monitored the recovery of Elsie Needham from a diabetic coma. He also admitted Charlotte Clarke as a candidate for insulin therapy, demonstrating his hands-on involvement in patient care during the experimental phase. In a testament to his commitment to safety, Banting tested the pancreatic extract on himself before it was administered to human patients. He further marked a historic milestone by delivering the first dose of insulin in the United States to Jim Havens, a young boy suffering from severe diabetes.

Beyond experimentation and clinical work, Banting played a crucial role in advancing the global acceptance of insulin therapy. He presented the team’s findings at medical conventions, facilitating rapid dissemination of the discovery within the medical community. Although initially reluctant to seek personal recognition, Banting eventually agreed to have his name included on the patent for insulin, formally acknowledging his indispensable contribution. His leadership, from securing research opportunities to conducting experiments, overseeing patient treatment, and promoting the therapy’s adoption, was central to one of the most transformative medical breakthroughs of the 20th century.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3b5ce540c7784f49191a479629af78e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c04d944f188525956166c3081e7e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1961d5ebd8cd94a8e08ca46c59210d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="University of Toronto">
  <data key="d0">University of Toronto</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The University of Toronto is a Canadian academic institution globally recognized for its central role in the discovery and early development of insulin, one of the most significant medical breakthroughs of the 20th century. In the 1920s, the university served as the research base for Frederick Banting and Charles Best, who conducted pioneering experiments on insulin under the guidance of Dr. John Macleod, a faculty member in the Department of Physiology. Their work, primarily carried out in the university’s Medical Building, involved collaboration across multiple departments, including Physiology and Pharmacology. James Bertram Collip and Peter A. T. Fitzgerald were also key contributors to the research team, with the collective efforts leading to the successful isolation of insulin for the treatment of diabetes.

Beyond its role as a research hub, the University of Toronto played a crucial institutional role in the scientific, legal, and commercial dimensions of insulin’s development. Recognizing the public health imperative, the university was advised to hold the patent rights to insulin and chose to do so with the intent of ensuring broad accessibility rather than maximizing profit. To facilitate large-scale production and distribution, the institution entered into agreements with pharmaceutical companies, most notably Eli Lilly and Company. Additionally, the university was instrumental in founding Connaught Laboratories—later known as Connaught Medical Research Laboratories—as a non-profit entity dedicated to producing insulin and advancing public health innovations.

Today, the University of Toronto remains deeply associated with the legacy of insulin’s discovery. It continues to preserve the historical documentation of this milestone and is recognized as the academic home where Banting, Best, Macleod, and their colleagues transformed medical science. The institution’s dual affiliation with both the foundational research and the institutional mechanisms that brought insulin to the world underscores its enduring impact on global health and biomedical research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69124f1cc2248b019af52b9d2e71c12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e08a876cbf5eb18e5ec55068b1a54d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bb6354e9611023f50fce6a399d619a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb1d6cf513719b7859327bdb2f78fee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3b5ce540c7784f49191a479629af78e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c04d944f188525956166c3081e7e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1961d5ebd8cd94a8e08ca46c59210d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Collip">
  <data key="d0">Collip</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Collip is a scientist who played a pivotal role in the development and purification of insulin during the early 20th century. He was a key member of the research team engaged in insulin research, working alongside notable figures such as Charles Best. Collip developed the vacuum still, a critical apparatus used in the preparation of whole beef pancreas extract, which enabled more effective isolation of insulin for experimental and clinical use. His technical innovation significantly advanced the efficiency and consistency of insulin extraction methods.

In addition to engineering the vacuum still, Collip discovered a method to purify insulin by carefully controlling alcohol concentration, a breakthrough that enhanced the potency and safety of insulin preparations. This refinement was instrumental in transitioning insulin from a crude extract to a medically viable treatment for diabetes. Collip contributed broadly to the insulin project by developing and improving techniques for both producing and testing the pancreatic extract, ensuring its reliability and therapeutic effectiveness.

Collip's contributions were formally recognized through his co-holding of the U.S. patent application for insulin with Best, underscoring his integral role in the discovery and development process. A landmark moment in his work occurred when an insulin preparation developed under his direction successfully revived an eight-year-old girl in Edmonton, demonstrating the life-saving potential of the hormone. This clinical success highlighted the practical impact of his scientific contributions and cemented his legacy in the history of medical science.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3b5ce540c7784f49191a479629af78e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f86120a59757edf737bd7d738968533&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643971</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Toronto Star">
  <data key="d0">Toronto Star</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Toronto Star is a newspaper that played a significant role in early public reporting on the discovery and development of insulin as a treatment for diabetes. On January 14, 1922, the publication released one of the first press accounts detailing progress in diabetes treatment, specifically highlighting the successful treatment of a young boy. This article marked the beginning of widespread media coverage on the medical breakthrough and brought public attention to the research being conducted by doctors in Toronto.

Further advancing public awareness, the Toronto Star published a key article on March 22, 1922, which reported on the discovery of insulin by a team of Toronto-based physicians. This coverage helped solidify public recognition of the scientific achievement and underscored the importance of the medical milestone. The newspaper continued its dedicated reporting throughout the year, providing additional media coverage on October 14, 1922, followed by the publication of an official statement on October 20, 1922. These reports collectively contributed to documenting and disseminating critical information about insulin’s development, establishing the Toronto Star as a central source of public information during a pivotal moment in medical history.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0eaeeaa0051cb78248f45d6af3009e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
