<node id="Macleod">
  <data key="d0">Macleod</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Macleod is a professor and senior researcher at the University of Toronto who played a central and multifaceted role in the discovery and development of insulin. As a supervisory figure in the landmark diabetes research project, he provided essential laboratory space and resources to Frederick Banting and Charles Best, arranged retroactive pay for Banting, and offered consistent scientific guidance throughout their experiments on pancreatic extracts. His leadership was characterized by a cautious and rigorous approach, as he repeatedly urged Banting and Best to validate their findings and focus on confirming the therapeutic effectiveness of their results. Banting frequently communicated with Macleod, reporting progress and awaiting instructions, underscoring Macleod’s authoritative and mentorship role in the research endeavor.

Macleod expanded the research team by inviting biochemist James Collip to join, ensuring the scientific and biochemical integrity of the work. He was a co-author of the seminal paper published in the Canadian Medical Association Journal, which detailed the discovery and medical potential of insulin, cementing his status as an integral member of the Toronto research team. During a presentation in Washington, D.C., Macleod named the pancreatic extract "insulin," a term that became universally adopted. His name was initially linked to the serum used in the first successful human treatment of Leonard Thompson, reflecting his perceived leadership in the project. Additionally, Macleod participated in pivotal decisions regarding the patenting and ethical dissemination of insulin, helping shape the framework for its scientific and public accessibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-030fb051231df38c20ff2e1c1e73153d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9864deffdfa4a86915e3d7985867376&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c04d944f188525956166c3081e7e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
