<node id="Pancreatic Extract">
  <data key="d0">Pancreatic Extract</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Pancreatic Extract is a substance developed by Frederick Banting and Charles Best in the early 1920s as part of their groundbreaking research into diabetes treatment. It was initially prepared from the degenerated pancreases of dogs following surgical ligation of the pancreatic duct, a method designed to atrophy the digestive cells while preserving the islet of Langerhans tissue, which was hypothesized to produce an internal secretion regulating blood sugar. This early preparation, derived specifically from canine pancreases, was used in experiments to treat diabetic dogs, where it demonstrated a significant ability to reduce blood glucose levels and alleviate clinical symptoms of diabetes.

As research progressed, the definition of Pancreatic Extract broadened to include preparations from the pancreases of various animals, not limited to dogs. The substance was used extensively in experimental settings to test its effects on blood sugar regulation in both diabetic animals and, later, human patients. Initially referred to as "Pancreatic Extract," it was subsequently named "Isletin" to reflect its origin in the islets of Langerhans. Its primary purpose was to isolate and study the pancreas's internal secretion, which played a crucial role in glucose metabolism.

The successful application of Pancreatic Extract in animal models paved the way for its use in human trials, where it similarly proved effective in controlling blood sugar levels in diabetic patients. These results were instrumental in confirming the biological activity of the pancreatic secretion and directly led to the purification and standardization of insulin as a therapeutic agent. Thus, Pancreatic Extract represents a foundational milestone in medical science—the first effective preparation that enabled the discovery and clinical development of insulin, revolutionizing the treatment of diabetes worldwide.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-030fb051231df38c20ff2e1c1e73153d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb1d6cf513719b7859327bdb2f78fee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c04d944f188525956166c3081e7e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f86120a59757edf737bd7d738968533</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
