<node id="Dog #410">
  <data key="d0">Dog #410</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Dog #410 was the first dog to receive an injection of a pancreatic extract on 30 July 1921, marking a pivotal moment in early diabetes research. This experimental procedure, conducted as part of pioneering efforts to isolate insulin, demonstrated a marked physiological effect, indicating the potential of the extract to influence blood sugar levels in diabetic subjects. The response observed in Dog #410 provided crucial preliminary evidence supporting the therapeutic potential of pancreatic secretions in treating diabetes.

Despite the promising initial reaction, Dog #410 died the following day, on 31 July 1921, after lapsing into a diabetic coma. The cause of death underscored the challenges and risks inherent in the early stages of insulin research, where dosage control and extract purity were not yet fully understood or standardized. As a test subject in these foundational experiments, Dog #410 played a significant role in the development of insulin therapy, contributing to subsequent refinements that would eventually lead to life-saving treatments for diabetes in humans. The repeated documentation of these events across multiple descriptions confirms the dog’s identity and fate without contradiction, highlighting its historical importance in medical science.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb1d6cf513719b7859327bdb2f78fee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Tipos de insulinas/The Discovery and Development.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643964</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
