</node>
<node id="ISO 15197:2013">
  <data key="d0">ISO 15197:2013</data>
  <data key="d1">standard</data>
  <data key="d2">ISO 15197:2013 is an international standard specifying accuracy requirements for blood glucose meters.&lt;SEP&gt;ISO 15197:2013 is a standard for blood glucose meter accuracy established by the International Organization for Standardization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c03aef84b96134fc1f0a2a4d8466e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8bc02d862fc2c83e40dafc871482c2c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643549</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FDA">
  <data key="d0">FDA</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal regulatory agency responsible for overseeing the safety, efficacy, and approval of a wide range of medical products and health technologies in the United States. It plays a central role in regulating medical devices, including blood glucose monitoring systems such as blood glucose meters and continuous glucose monitors (CGMs). The FDA establishes accuracy standards for these devices and designates certain CGMs as integrated, enabling their use with automated insulin delivery systems. Additionally, the agency approves insulin delivery devices, sensor-augmented pumps with low glucose suspend capabilities, and bolus calculators, ensuring their safe integration into diabetes management.

Beyond diabetes-related technologies, the FDA is responsible for evaluating and approving clinically validated digital health tools, including digital therapeutics. This reflects its broader mandate to monitor and authorize innovative medical solutions that meet rigorous clinical standards. The agency also oversees pharmaceuticals, as demonstrated by its approval of teplizumab, a monoclonal antibody used to delay the onset of Stage 3 Type 1 Diabetes in high-risk individuals. Multiple descriptions confirm this approval, emphasizing the FDA’s role in advancing preventive treatments for autoimmune conditions like type 1 diabetes. Through its regulatory oversight, the FDA ensures that medical devices, digital health technologies, and therapeutics meet established benchmarks for safety and effectiveness before reaching patients and healthcare providers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c03aef84b96134fc1f0a2a4d8466e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b94e98f4cb6f97fceae5e6d8c30c871b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-418c63d7f8a7a0408d9107e40d9251db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f357ebc59eb6ed3dd2035a13da0df52f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ff5070c8f5a6109621d29aa2b13398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dad8318811ce10b7f639b13057d611f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-309ce40527b1cc8f260e96e195742001</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689024</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hospital Use">
  <data key="d0">Hospital Use</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hospital use refers to the application of blood glucose meters in clinical settings with stricter accuracy requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c03aef84b96134fc1f0a2a4d8466e0d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
