<node id="Consensus Error Grid">
  <data key="d0">Consensus Error Grid</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The consensus error grid is a tool used to evaluate the clinical accuracy of blood glucose meters, where values in the A and B regions are considered clinically acceptable.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical tool developed to evaluate the significance of inaccuracies in blood glucose measurement, introduced in a 2000 Diabetes Care paper.&lt;SEP&gt;The consensus error grid is an analytical tool used to evaluate the clinical accuracy and safety of blood glucose monitoring systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c03aef84b96134fc1f0a2a4d8466e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b4ef00b7799130b85ed6433c381dfef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498e4bb45447ec837c66b8fa72136a1b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776643551</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flash Glucose Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">Flash Glucose Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Flash glucose monitoring is a system that allows users to scan a sensor to obtain interstitial glucose readings without routine fingersticks.&lt;SEP&gt;Flash glucose monitoring is a form of intermittently scanned continuous glucose monitoring used for managing diabetes, associated with improved glycemic outcomes and patient satisfaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Flash glucose monitoring is a technique used to monitor glucose levels in patients with diabetes, shown to improve glycemic control in multiple studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Flash glucose monitoring is a system where a sensor is worn on the skin and requires a reader to scan it for glucose data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f4e05859597c5f1693c2f9fe1ef0a02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-755e9677a7018e4cc382b7d903590786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07c7e3d6615d1076ae20653358419ff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22078404beeceb5adc3e688d239c585e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1776644587</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hba1c">
  <data key="d0">Hba1c</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">HbA1c is a measure of long-term blood glucose control, often used to assess the effectiveness of diabetes management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) is a blood marker used to assess long-term glucose levels and is used in the diagnosis and classification of diabetes and prediabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;HbA1c is a blood test that measures average blood glucose levels and is used as a predictor for diabetes and an outcome in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;HbA1c is a diagnostic method for diabetes mellitus that measures glycated hemoglobin levels but has lower diagnostic sensitivity and does not account for individual variability in protein glycation.&lt;SEP&gt;HbA1c is a diagnostic method for diabetes mellitus that measures glycated hemoglobin levels but has lower diagnostic sensitivity and does not account for individual variability in protein glycation.&lt;SEP&gt;HbA1c is a measure of average blood glucose levels over time and is used as a key metric in diabetes management, with specific values linked to risks of mortality and complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f4e05859597c5f1693c2f9fe1ef0a02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a775efb764958cf67853f275782e2a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3d27a8dbb6882ee6a732de5ac202d64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564f10440969233a515af4974c158c4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02fb9dc2bae2939b212aa5df09194ee4</data>
