<node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration regulates medical devices and ensures the accuracy and safety of blood glucose meters and other diabetes technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration regulates the approval and accuracy standards for glucose monitoring devices.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration is a regulatory agency that has approved and expanded indications for continuous glucose monitoring systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration issued a news release expanding the indication for a continuous glucose monitoring system to replace fingerstick testing.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration is a regulatory agency that issued enforcement policies and guidance documents for remote glucose monitoring devices during the COVID-19 public health emergency.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approves point-of-care A1C testing devices and regulates their use in clinical settings.</data>
