<node id="Ann Intern Med">
  <data key="d0">Ann Intern Med</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Ann Intern Med is a medical journal that has published a range of influential studies related to diabetes, glycemic measurement, and metabolic health. In 2009, it published the original diabetes risk algorithm developed by Bang et al., which contributed to early risk prediction models for diabetes. The journal further advanced research in diabetes diagnostics by publishing studies on glycemia measures and their role in diabetes diagnosis, helping to refine clinical criteria and testing methodologies.

Ann Intern Med also played a key role in evaluating diabetes management technologies and practices. In 2012, it published a meta-analysis summarizing the effectiveness of self-monitoring of blood glucose in non-insulin-treated type 2 diabetes, providing critical insights into its limited clinical benefit in that population. Later, the journal featured findings from the DIAMOND Study Group, presenting results from a randomized trial that compared continuous glucose monitoring to usual care in individuals with type 2 diabetes, highlighting emerging technologies in diabetes management.

Additionally, the journal has addressed health disparities and biological variations in diabetes markers. It published research on glucose-independent racial differences in hemoglobin A1c levels and broader racial differences in glycemic markers, contributing to a deeper understanding of how race and ethnicity may influence diagnostic accuracy and clinical interpretation of blood sugar measures. In 2023, Ann Intern Med published a study examining the prevalence and implications of obesity among U.S. adults with type 1 diabetes, expanding the discourse on metabolic comorbidities in populations traditionally associated with different diabetes types. Collectively, these publications underscore Ann Intern Med’s role as a leading venue for high-impact, clinically relevant diabetes research.</data>
