<node id="Glycated Hemoglobin">
  <data key="d0">Glycated Hemoglobin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) is a measure of long-term blood glucose control, used as an outcome indicator in diabetes studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycated hemoglobin is a form of hemoglobin used as a marker for long-term blood glucose levels and associated with cardiovascular and kidney disease risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycated hemoglobin is a biomarker studied for its association with diabetes and cardiovascular risk in non-diabetic adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycated hemoglobin is a biomarker studied for its association with diabetes and cardiovascular risk in non-diabetic adults.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07c7e3d6615d1076ae20653358419ff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05070e0755e36e3375d401400cf75fce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f017257ae0e7fcc5a0a1be0ddc435fe8</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/blood glucose monitoring.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689016</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gestational Diabetes Mellitus">
  <data key="d0">Gestational Diabetes Mellitus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is a form of diabetes that develops during pregnancy, characterized by glucose intolerance or elevated blood glucose levels first recognized during gestation. It is not pre-existing or overt diabetes but is typically diagnosed in the second or third trimester, most commonly between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy. Screening for GDM is a routine component of prenatal care and is usually conducted through glucose tolerance tests, with additional monitoring involving HbA1c and other glucose assessments to manage blood sugar levels effectively.

GDM poses significant risks to both maternal and neonatal health. Maternal complications may include preeclampsia and an increased likelihood of cesarean delivery, while fetal and neonatal risks encompass macrosomia, neonatal hypoglycemia, and other adverse perinatal outcomes. Although GDM generally resolves after childbirth, it has important long-term health implications. Women with a history of GDM face a substantially higher risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life, and their children are at increased risk for obesity and metabolic disorders. As such, GDM is considered a temporary yet clinically significant condition requiring careful management during pregnancy and ongoing health surveillance for both mother and child beyond delivery. Early identification through routine glucose screening is critical to optimizing health outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07c7e3d6615d1076ae20653358419ff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1f5c2bbe2ca0e80b85850ca2f9a5c84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6b2f90b1ec83426a0df84d7693bc26d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f5ebd46f8491409b5f07f5c83542afd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f5cd98bab9909442c09e3fcd5b21fda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45a8ca206e61b47964f899c929fbafb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f182d57176633ee05e1a23031d6ad2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa8638b27cbd181b507c81fea49c6f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fab467dd1cac7fbb17a945a0aa8240f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050dc3a498d3027939b93706cbdb7f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4f56a727625747d95bd88ea9712defc</data>
