</node>
<node id="IGT">
  <data key="d0">IGT</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">IGT stands for impaired glucose tolerance, defined by 2-hour plasma glucose levels of 140–199 mg/dL during a 75-g OGTT, and is a form of prediabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;IGT stands for impaired glucose tolerance, defined by 2-hour plasma glucose levels of 140–199 mg/dL during a 75-g OGTT, and is a form of prediabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647373</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IFG">
  <data key="d0">IFG</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">IFG stands for impaired fasting glucose, defined as fasting plasma glucose levels of 100–125 mg/dL (5.6–6.9 mmol/L), and is a form of prediabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;IFG stands for impaired fasting glucose, defined as fasting plasma glucose levels of 100–125 mg/dL (5.6–6.9 mmol/L), and is a form of prediabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647373</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GDM">
  <data key="d0">GDM</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is a condition characterized by high blood glucose levels during pregnancy. It occurs when the body cannot produce enough insulin to meet the increased demands of pregnancy, leading to elevated blood sugar. GDM is typically diagnosed through standardized screening protocols, with common diagnostic criteria including those established by the International Association of the Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups (IADPSG) and the Carpenter-Coustan guidelines. These criteria help identify affected individuals based on glucose tolerance test results during prenatal care.

The condition poses health risks not only during pregnancy but also in the long term. GDM increases the likelihood of complications for both the mother and the infant, such as preeclampsia, cesarean delivery, macrosomia (large birth weight), and neonatal hypoglycemia. Furthermore, women who experience gestational diabetes mellitus have a significantly higher risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life. Close monitoring and management during pregnancy, often involving dietary changes, physical activity, and sometimes insulin therapy, are essential to minimize adverse outcomes. Postpartum follow-up is also recommended to assess glucose metabolism and reduce future diabetes risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-357f19fa58c4dd99dcd7b47a984b4383&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7869f89a9c58ace31efe679a36aab7cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d936160aac8a6b8ba40b196280422fe5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obesity">
  <data key="d0">Obesity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Obesity is a medical condition defined by excessive accumulation of body fat, which significantly elevates the risk of developing a range of metabolic disorders. It is strongly associated with insulin resistance, a pathological state in which cells fail to respond appropriately to insulin, and serves as a primary risk factor for the development of type 2 diabetes mellitus (DM2). This relationship is particularly pronounced in children and adolescents, where 70% to 90% of those diagnosed with type 2 diabetes are obese, and 38% suffer from severe obesity.

Abdominal or visceral obesity, a subtype characterized by fat accumulation around internal organs, is especially linked to prediabetes and the progression toward metabolic dysfunction. The condition plays a central role in a broader pathological continuum that includes metabolic syndrome and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), both of which further amplify the risk of type 2 diabetes. Due to its strong association with insulin resistance and metabolic complications, obesity is routinely used in clinical settings to help differentiate type 2 diabetes from type 1 diabetes, which is not typically linked to body weight or adiposity. Overall, obesity is a critical public health concern due to its pervasive influence on metabolic health and its contribution to the rising incidence of chronic diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a major risk factor for DM2 in children and adolescents, with 70% to 90% of affected patients being obese and 38% having severe obesity.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a key factor in the continuum leading to metabolic syndrome, NAFLD, and type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a major risk factor for DM2 in children and adolescents, with 70% to 90% of affected patients being obese and 38% having severe obesity.&lt;SEP&gt;Obesity is a key factor in the continuum leading to metabolic syndrome, NAFLD, and type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a874190e40ad535ac9b317a469571d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fff9929a18d525e3805a8ed08356d41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e35953dbfdebbbab4bf83ec032ccfb7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd83fee2b80ad551bb282975932751ce</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classificação do diabetes.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688987</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="World Health Organization">
  <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The World Health Organization (WHO) is a leading global health authority responsible for establishing and disseminating international guidelines and diagnostic criteria for various health conditions, particularly diabetes and related metabolic disorders. It plays a central role in defining standardized diagnostic thresholds, including those for impaired fasting glucose, which the WHO sets at a lower limit of 110 mg/dL (6.1 mmol/L). The organization also provides detailed protocols for conducting the oral glucose tolerance test, a key diagnostic tool in assessing glucose metabolism and diagnosing diabetes.

In addition to diagnostic standards, the World Health Organization addresses population-specific health risks through tailored guidelines. It has issued recommendations on body mass index (BMI) thresholds to identify increased diabetes risk across diverse populations. Notably, the WHO convened an expert consultation to evaluate appropriate BMI classifications for Asian populations, recognizing the need for region-specific criteria due to differences in body composition and disease risk, and to inform public health policy accordingly.

The World Health Organization also contributes to the classification and understanding of diabetes subtypes. It recognizes Latent Autoimmune Diabetes in Adults (LADA) as a hybrid form of diabetes that exhibits features of both Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes. Furthermore, in 2019, the WHO published an updated classification of diabetes mellitus under the CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO license, reinforcing its role in shaping global diabetes nomenclature and clinical guidance. Collectively, these efforts underscore the WHO’s comprehensive involvement in diabetes research, diagnosis, and policy development on an international scale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8317327cb2c248762b2dc3852c662000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b5fc9e82121669cb9d4aff08c8c9019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15a18ecfcb20fceb4fd80ab22186964e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00f6ea581f76ddc15bc463d1eccc4496&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c51943f25319629d0e145186a85c3b59</data>
