</node>
<node id="BMI">
  <data key="d0">BMI</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Body Mass Index (BMI) is a measure of body fat based on an individual's height and weight, widely used to assess weight status and classify overweight and obesity in both children and adults. It serves as a practical tool for evaluating nutritional status and is calculated as weight in kilograms divided by the square of height in meters (kg/m²). For adults, a BMI of ≥25 kg/m² is generally considered overweight, and ≥30 kg/m² indicates obesity, with increased health risks. Notably, lower thresholds are recommended for certain populations, such as Asian Americans, for whom a BMI of ≥23 kg/m² is considered a risk factor for conditions like type 2 diabetes and prediabetes, reflecting ethnic differences in body composition and disease risk.

In pediatric populations, BMI is assessed using age- and sex-specific percentiles rather than fixed cutoffs, allowing for appropriate classification of weight status relative to growth patterns. Elevated BMI is recognized as a significant risk factor for multiple health conditions, particularly type 2 diabetes and prediabetes. While obesity is commonly associated with type 2 diabetes, BMI also plays a role in diabetes classification more broadly; a substantial proportion of adults diagnosed with type 1 diabetes are overweight or obese, indicating that high BMI does not exclude type 1 diagnosis. Additionally, elevated BMI is linked to an increased risk of post-transplantation diabetes mellitus (PTDM) following organ transplantation, highlighting its relevance in clinical outcomes for immunocompromised patients.

BMI is also a target for therapeutic intervention in specific medical contexts, such as cystic fibrosis-related diabetes, where improving nutritional status through insulin therapy aims to increase BMI and support overall health. Despite its widespread use, BMI is a surrogate measure of body fat and does not directly assess fat distribution or muscle mass, yet it remains a valuable, accessible tool in public health and clinical practice for identifying individuals at risk for metabolic and cardiovascular diseases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc7d59e52d23bbcf0d499f8423581f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8317327cb2c248762b2dc3852c662000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c85dba4b510b2463c6766fd8a2dbf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3bdf442194477639404123cea5d5974&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb968add601368451243acd6b2322ce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e575f95ec5a02f7900989e00da53749f</data>
