</node>
<node id="Fasting Plasma Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Fasting Plasma Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fasting Plasma Glucose (FPG) is a blood test that measures the concentration of glucose in the plasma after an overnight fast, typically lasting at least 8 hours. It is primarily used as a diagnostic and screening tool for diabetes, including type 2 diabetes and prediabetes. A fasting plasma glucose level of ≥126 mg/dL is recognized as a diagnostic criterion for diabetes, while intermediate values may indicate prediabetes, allowing for early intervention and management.

In addition to its role in diagnosing general diabetes, Fasting Plasma Glucose is also utilized in prenatal care to screen for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM). During the first prenatal visit in China, this test is routinely administered to assess the risk of GDM, with specific cutoff values guiding diagnosis. Screening thresholds for GDM are generally lower than those for diabetes, with cutoffs set at either 85 mg/dL or 90 mg/dL depending on clinical guidelines and risk factors.

Overall, Fasting Plasma Glucose serves as a simple, cost-effective, and widely adopted method for evaluating glucose metabolism in both general and pregnant populations. Its standardized procedure and clear diagnostic thresholds make it a cornerstone in the early detection and management of various forms of diabetes, supporting timely clinical decision-making and preventive care.&lt;SEP&gt;Fasting Plasma Glucose (GJ) is a blood test measuring glucose levels after fasting, used as a diagnostic criterion for diabetes and prediabetes.&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Fasting Plasma Glucose (GJ) is a blood test measuring glucose levels after fasting, used as a diagnostic criterion for diabetes and prediabetes.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20b3e88dda40e2d303af38aea0d19b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b5fc9e82121669cb9d4aff08c8c9019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fab467dd1cac7fbb17a945a0aa8240f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3bdf442194477639404123cea5d5974&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d936160aac8a6b8ba40b196280422fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcce093409f85a2fbec9a754f86c8e4e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2-Hour Plasma Glucose">
  <data key="d0">2-Hour Plasma Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">2-Hour Plasma Glucose (2-h PG) is a clinical measurement used to assess blood glucose levels two hours after the ingestion of a glucose solution during a 75-gram oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT). This test is a standardized diagnostic procedure in which an individual consumes a glucose load, and their plasma glucose concentration is measured at the two-hour mark to evaluate how effectively the body processes glucose.

The 2-hour plasma glucose measurement serves as a key diagnostic criterion for diabetes mellitus. A 2-h PG level of ≥200 mg/dL during the OGTT is indicative of diabetes, according to established clinical guidelines. In addition to diagnosing diabetes, this test is also used to identify impaired glucose tolerance (IGT), a prediabetic state that reflects abnormal glucose metabolism and increases the risk of developing type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease.

Multiple descriptions consistently emphasize the role of the 2-hour plasma glucose test in diabetes diagnosis and glucose metabolism assessment, with no conflicting definitions or interpretations present. The test is uniformly described as part of the OGTT protocol, reinforcing its reliability and standardized application in clinical practice. Overall, 2-hour plasma glucose is a critical biomarker in the evaluation of glycemic control and metabolic health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20b3e88dda40e2d303af38aea0d19b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b5fc9e82121669cb9d4aff08c8c9019</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647320</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Impaired Glucose Tolerance">
  <data key="d0">Impaired Glucose Tolerance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is a prediabetic state associated with increased risk for type 2 diabetes and is defined by a 2-hour plasma glucose value of 140–199 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is a prediabetic condition where 2-hour plasma glucose levels during an OGTT are between 140 mg/dL and 199 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired glucose tolerance is a pre-diabetic state associated with an increased risk of developing type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is a prediabetic state associated with increased risk for type 2 diabetes and is defined by a 2-hour plasma glucose value of 140–199 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is a prediabetic condition where 2-hour plasma glucose levels during an OGTT are between 140 mg/dL and 199 mg/dL.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired glucose tolerance is a pre-diabetic state associated with an increased risk of developing type 2 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3d27a8dbb6882ee6a732de5ac202d64</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Impaired Fasting Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Impaired Fasting Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG) is a prediabetic condition characterized by elevated blood glucose levels following a period of fasting, indicating an intermediate state between normal glucose metabolism and type 2 diabetes. It is clinically defined by a fasting plasma glucose value ranging from 100 mg/dL to 125 mg/dL, a threshold established by medical guidelines to identify individuals at increased risk for developing diabetes. This condition reflects impaired glucose regulation, where the body's ability to manage blood sugar effectively begins to decline, though not to the extent observed in full-blown diabetes.

Individuals with Impaired Fasting Glucose exhibit higher-than-normal glucose levels after an overnight fast, signaling early metabolic dysfunction. The condition is often identified during routine blood testing and serves as a critical marker for assessing diabetes risk. Research and clinical studies frequently use fasting plasma glucose thresholds to evaluate and stratify individuals with IFG, enabling targeted interventions for diabetes prevention. However, when considered in isolation, IFG is considered less predictive of successful diabetes prevention outcomes compared to other diagnostic criteria, such as impaired glucose tolerance or HbA1c levels, highlighting the importance of comprehensive metabolic assessment.

As a pre-diabetic state, Impaired Fasting Glucose carries significant implications for long-term health, including an increased likelihood of progressing to type 2 diabetes, as well as a heightened risk for cardiovascular complications. Early detection and lifestyle modifications—such as improved diet, increased physical activity, and weight management—are commonly recommended to mitigate disease progression. Overall, IFG serves as an important warning sign, offering a window of opportunity for intervention to delay or prevent the onset of diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired fasting glucose is a prediabetic state characterized by higher-than-normal blood glucose levels after fasting.&lt;SEP&gt;Impaired fasting glucose is a pre-diabetic state with implications for diabetes risk, studied using fasting plasma glucose thresholds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f017257ae0e7fcc5a0a1be0ddc435fe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2977bb9a41689e74de2c536343624744</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688979</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program">
  <data key="d0">National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) certifies A1C testing methods to ensure standardization with the DCCT assay.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) certifies A1C testing methods and ensures they are standardized to the DCCT assay.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) certifies A1C testing methods to ensure standardization with the DCCT assay.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) certifies A1C testing methods and ensures they are standardized to the DCCT assay.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) certifies the validation of HbA1c testing methods for clinical applicability.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) certifies the validation of HbA1c testing methods for clinical applicability.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program (NGSP) is an organization that certifies laboratory methods for HbA1c testing and defines its normal range as values below 5.7%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b5fc9e82121669cb9d4aff08c8c9019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564f10440969233a515af4974c158c4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f7252ca9147660b04b7329edbe843f</data>
