  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IPEX Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">IPEX Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">IPEX Syndrome (Immunodysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked) is a rare, early-onset systemic autoimmune genetic disorder caused by mutations in the FOXP3 gene. As an X-linked condition, it primarily affects males and is characterized by a triad of immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, and enteropathy. The dysfunction of regulatory T cells due to FOXP3 mutations leads to a breakdown in immune tolerance, resulting in widespread autoimmunity.

The clinical manifestations of IPEX Syndrome commonly include autoimmune enteropathy, which presents as severe diarrhea and malabsorption, often appearing in infancy. Endocrine abnormalities are also prominent, with autoimmune type 1 diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease—such as autoimmune thyroiditis—being frequently observed. Additionally, dermatological involvement is common, with patients often developing eczematous or psoriasiform dermatitis.

As a monogenic disorder, IPEX Syndrome provides a clear genetic basis for systemic autoimmunity, with the FOXP3 gene playing a critical role in the development and function of regulatory T cells. The syndrome is consistently associated with multiple autoimmune conditions, underscoring its systemic nature. Due to its rarity and severity, early diagnosis and interventions such as immunosuppressive therapy or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation are crucial for improving outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30c37422889250d7e58837deb17406d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620e2d1316d6093170f1c9991caabc7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0510d1781e9c07bebd7a7422d453cbf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-311745e1d3e05390ed93d1e683c07243</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FOXP3 Gene">
  <data key="d0">FOXP3 Gene</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The FOXP3 gene encodes the forkhead box protein 3, and its mutation causes IPEX syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;The FOXP3 gene is associated with IPEX syndrome, a genetic disorder causing early-onset systemic autoimmunity linked to type 1 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;The FOXP3 gene encodes the forkhead box protein 3, and its mutation causes IPEX syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;The FOXP3 gene is associated with IPEX syndrome, a genetic disorder causing early-onset systemic autoimmunity linked to type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30c37422889250d7e58837deb17406d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620e2d1316d6093170f1c9991caabc7e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776647375</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oral Glucose Tolerance Test">
  <data key="d0">Oral Glucose Tolerance Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT), also known as TTGO, is a diagnostic procedure used to evaluate the body's ability to metabolize glucose by measuring blood or plasma glucose levels before and at intervals after the ingestion of a glucose solution. The standard form of the test typically involves the administration of a 75-gram glucose load, with glucose levels measured 2 hours afterward to assess glucose metabolism, aiding in the diagnosis of type 2 diabetes and prediabetes. It is recognized as a highly sensitive method for detecting early metabolic abnormalities, often considered the gold standard in certain clinical settings due to its superior sensitivity compared to alternatives like Hemoglobin A1c, particularly in early detection of dysglycemia.

In addition to its role in diagnosing type 2 diabetes and prediabetes, the OGTT is widely used in specialized clinical contexts. It is the recommended method for annual screening of cystic fibrosis–related diabetes in individuals aged 10 years and older, and it is the preferred diagnostic tool for confirming posttransplantation diabetes mellitus. The test is also commonly employed in the diagnosis of gestational diabetes, highlighting its broad utility across various forms of glucose metabolism disorders. By assessing insulin sensitivity and diabetes risk through dynamic glucose monitoring, the OGTT provides a comprehensive evaluation of metabolic function.

Notably, there is emerging guidance suggesting that the 1-hour OGTT (TTGO-1h) may be superior and more practical than the traditional 2-hour test (TTGO-2h) for diagnosing type 2 diabetes and prediabetes, indicating a potential shift in clinical preference toward earlier time-point assessment. Despite variations in protocol, the core principle of the OGTT remains consistent: to provoke and observe the body’s glycemic response under controlled conditions. As a result, the Oral Glucose Tolerance Test remains an indispensable tool in both routine endocrinological practice and specialized screening programs, essential for preventing underdiagnosis and enabling timely intervention in individuals at risk for diabetes and related metabolic conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b36fc8288de16b0682d3530712dcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f5ebd46f8491409b5f07f5c83542afd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53f409a49620f4d40ad687bd0c0df19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a862c4dc9595df3958bd7626f7145d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-836e865d51bd855e9d073bd7a5ac7657&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b5fc9e82121669cb9d4aff08c8c9019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3d27a8dbb6882ee6a732de5ac202d64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3bdf442194477639404123cea5d5974&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd2e74b5f8450784b7c60acd6751ae14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb9c311fc732ddd262c56ddc696da954&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0510d1781e9c07bebd7a7422d453cbf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb968add601368451243acd6b2322ce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564f10440969233a515af4974c158c4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bc23291d65efde6aaf97a925c92e183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2977bb9a41689e74de2c536343624744</data>
