<node id="Monogenic Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Monogenic Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Monogenic Diabetes is a rare form of diabetes caused by mutations in a single gene, leading to pancreatic beta-cell dysfunction. It encompasses specific subtypes such as maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) and neonatal diabetes, which typically manifest early in life—often before the age of 25. This condition is frequently misdiagnosed as either type 1 or type 2 diabetes due to overlapping clinical features, complicating accurate differential diagnosis. However, distinguishing characteristics such as the presence of detectable C-peptide levels above 0.6 ng/ml even after five years of diagnosis, absence of diabetes-related autoantibodies, and a strong family history of diabetes across two or three generations can support clinical suspicion of monogenic diabetes.

Diagnosis of Monogenic Diabetes relies on a combination of clinical criteria and genetic testing. It is often suspected in individuals presenting with hyperglycemia at a young age, particularly when accompanied by a familial pattern of inheritance consistent with autosomal dominant transmission. Unlike typical type 1 diabetes, where C-peptide levels are usually undetectable after several years, patients with monogenic forms may retain significant endogenous insulin production. This preservation of beta-cell function underscores the importance of accurate classification, as treatment strategies can differ significantly—some patients may successfully transition from insulin therapy to oral sulfonylureas, especially in cases linked to specific gene mutations such as those in the KCNJ11 or ABCC8 genes.

Overall, Monogenic Diabetes represents a genetically and clinically heterogeneous group of disorders that require careful evaluation to ensure proper management. Its rarity and phenotypic overlap with more common forms of diabetes necessitate a high index of suspicion, particularly in atypical presentations. Genetic and clinical evaluation is essential for confirming the diagnosis, enabling personalized treatment approaches, and facilitating family screening.&lt;SEP&gt;Monogenic diabetes refers to forms of diabetes caused by single-gene mutations, including neonatal diabetes and MODY, leading to b-cell dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Monogenic diabetes is a form of diabetes caused by mutations in a single gene, often misdiagnosed as type 1 or type 2 diabetes, and includes MODY and neonatal diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Monogenic diabetes is a form of diabetes caused by single gene mutations, including subtypes such as MODY and neonatal diabetes, often misdiagnosed as type 1 or type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of diabetes caused by single gene mutations, often diagnosed in young individuals, including subtypes like MODY and neonatal diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0c65f0481b400f36e5caa3f51fabe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2773e1fe0d693b781b10643e037fc085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f780163f31c33c2bd7449fb5a87b42ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-311745e1d3e05390ed93d1e683c07243&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fff9929a18d525e3805a8ed08356d41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e575f95ec5a02f7900989e00da53749f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb7f10473b84f2de23852febe7ef317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f87c8aceb9340a61d8283da6282edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c85dba4b510b2463c6766fd8a2dbf5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classificação do diabetes.pdf</data>
