</node>
<node id="Neonatal Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Neonatal Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Neonatal diabetes is a rare form of monogenic diabetes that manifests in infants within the first six months of life, characterized by persistent hyperglycemia in the absence of evident secondary causes. It is distinguished from other forms of diabetes by its early onset and underlying genetic etiology, with diagnosis typically confirmed when hyperglycemia presents before the age of six months. Genetic screening is recommended for affected infants to identify specific mutations and guide appropriate management.

The condition can be either transient, resolving during infancy but potentially recurring later in life, or permanent, requiring lifelong treatment. Neonatal diabetes is commonly associated with mutations in specific genes, including KCNJ11, ABCC8, and INS, which play critical roles in pancreatic beta-cell function and insulin secretion. In some cases, de novo mutations in genes such as EIF2B1 have also been implicated, highlighting the genetic heterogeneity of the disorder. These genetic insights have significantly advanced both diagnosis and treatment strategies.

A key advancement in clinical management is the identification of patients who can transition from insulin therapy to oral sulfonylureas, particularly those with mutations in KCNJ11 or ABCC8. Sulfonylureas can effectively stimulate insulin release in these individuals by targeting the defective ATP-sensitive potassium channels in pancreatic beta cells, offering improved glycemic control and quality of life. The ability to tailor treatment based on genetic findings underscores the importance of early genetic testing in infants diagnosed with diabetes before six months of age.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0c65f0481b400f36e5caa3f51fabe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c85dba4b510b2463c6766fd8a2dbf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2773e1fe0d693b781b10643e037fc085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f780163f31c33c2bd7449fb5a87b42ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c51943f25319629d0e145186a85c3b59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad3acc8463b5ff47461b1c16ae2559cb</data>
