<node id="Selvin E">
  <data key="d0">Selvin E</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Selvin E is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on diabetes, glycemic markers, and their broader clinical and public health implications. A significant portion of Selvin E's research involves the study of glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c or A1C), including its performance in classifying and predicting diabetes, its short-term variability, and its utility in single-sample confirmatory testing for undiagnosed diabetes. This body of work contributes to refining diagnostic criteria and improving the accuracy of diabetes detection in clinical settings.

Selvin E has also investigated the association between glycated hemoglobin and various health outcomes, particularly cardiovascular disease and kidney disease, highlighting the prognostic value of HbA1c beyond glucose control. Additionally, Selvin E has explored racial differences in glycemic markers, examining how variations in HbA1c levels across populations may affect diabetes diagnosis and management, thereby addressing important health disparities.

Furthermore, Selvin E has contributed to research on obesity in U.S. adults with type 1 diabetes, analyzing its prevalence and implications within this population. This research underscores the complex interplay between body weight and diabetes outcomes, even in type 1 diabetes, which is traditionally considered distinct from obesity-related metabolic factors. Collectively, Selvin E's research spans epidemiological, diagnostic, and clinical domains, aiming to enhance understanding of diabetes risk, classification, and long-term outcomes through rigorous analysis of biomarkers and population data.&lt;SEP&gt;Selvin E is a researcher who evaluated the performance of A1C in classifying and predicting diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Selvin E is a researcher who studied short-term variability in glycemia measures and their impact on diabetes classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Selvin E is a researcher who studied glycated hemoglobin and its association with diabetes and cardiovascular risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Selvin E is a researcher who evaluated the performance of A1C in classifying and predicting diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Selvin E is a researcher who studied short-term variability in glycemia measures and their impact on diabetes classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Selvin E is a researcher who studied glycated hemoglobin and its association with diabetes and cardiovascular risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccde7bd12284b1c344a961b0f935b651&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb204c2d596853e5abff6c8dd0938ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05070e0755e36e3375d401400cf75fce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0510d1781e9c07bebd7a7422d453cbf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050dc3a498d3027939b93706cbdb7f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bc23291d65efde6aaf97a925c92e183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f017257ae0e7fcc5a0a1be0ddc435fe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a796990fe8edf70e4463124a0233c32c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classificação do diabetes.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Classification and Diagnosis of.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
