<node id="Consensus Guidance For Monitoring Individuals With Islet Autoantibody-Positive Pre-Stage 3 Type 1 Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Consensus Guidance For Monitoring Individuals With Islet Autoantibody-Positive Pre-Stage 3 Type 1 Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A consensus document providing clinical recommendations for monitoring individuals who are at risk for type 1 diabetes due to the presence of islet autoantibodies and are in pre-stage 3 of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A consensus document providing clinical recommendations for monitoring individuals who are at risk for type 1 diabetes due to the presence of islet autoantibodies and are in pre-stage 3 of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A consensus document providing clinical recommendations for monitoring individuals who are at risk for type 1 diabetes due to the presence of islet autoantibodies and are in pre-stage 3 of the disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cc90e1dea7f4271dde1545434c62508</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Screening For Type 1 Diabetes In The General Population">
  <data key="d0">Screening For Type 1 Diabetes In The General Population</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A proposed public health strategy to identify individuals at risk for type 1 diabetes before clinical onset, discussed in a status report by the NIDDK Type 1 Diabetes TrialNet Study Group.&lt;SEP&gt;A proposed public health strategy to identify individuals at risk for type 1 diabetes before clinical onset, discussed in a status report by the NIDDK Type 1 Diabetes TrialNet Study Group.&lt;SEP&gt;A proposed public health strategy to identify individuals at risk for type 1 diabetes before clinical onset, discussed in a status report by the NIDDK Type 1 Diabetes TrialNet Study Group.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cc90e1dea7f4271dde1545434c62508</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TTGO">
  <data key="d0">TTGO</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TTGO refers to the oral glucose tolerance test, used to diagnose diabetes and prediabetes by measuring blood glucose response after glucose ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO, or oral glucose tolerance test, is a diagnostic method used to detect diabetes mellitus, including cases not identified by fasting glucose or HbA1c.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO is the oral glucose tolerance test, used to evaluate glucose metabolism, with measurements taken at 1 or 2 hours post-consumption to assess diabetes risk.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO refers to the oral glucose tolerance test, used to diagnose diabetes and prediabetes by measuring blood glucose response after glucose ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO, or oral glucose tolerance test, is a diagnostic method used to detect diabetes mellitus, including cases not identified by fasting glucose or HbA1c.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO is the oral glucose tolerance test, used to evaluate glucose metabolism, with measurements taken at 1 or 2 hours post-consumption to assess diabetes risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d8f51fe0c33fe28ed188c855a82a3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TTGO-1h">
  <data key="d0">TTGO-1h</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TTGO-1h refers to the 1-hour oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), a diagnostic and predictive tool used in the assessment of type 2 diabetes mellitus (DM2). This test involves the measurement of blood glucose levels exactly one hour after the ingestion of a standardized glucose solution. It is considered a practical alternative to the traditional 2-hour OGTT, offering comparable or improved sensitivity in identifying individuals at risk for diabetes while reducing the time burden on patients and healthcare providers.

The primary clinical utility of TTGO-1h lies in its ability to detect undiagnosed type 2 diabetes and to predict future diabetes risk, particularly in high-risk populations. A glucose level of 209 mg/dL or higher at the 1-hour mark is recognized as a diagnostic cutoff for diabetes mellitus, making this threshold a critical benchmark in clinical evaluation. Due to its higher sensitivity compared to other glycemic markers, such as fasting glucose or HbA1c, TTGO-1h is increasingly favored for early detection of dysglycemia and impaired glucose metabolism.

Multiple descriptions consistently highlight the role of TTGO-1h in early risk prediction and its growing preference in clinical and research settings. By identifying abnormal glucose responses sooner, it enables timely interventions that may delay or prevent the onset of type 2 diabetes. Overall, TTGO-1h serves as a valuable, efficient, and sensitive tool in diabetes screening protocols, supporting proactive management of metabolic health.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-1h refers to the 1-hour oral glucose tolerance test, which is considered more practical than the 2-hour version and has a diagnostic cutoff of 209 mg/dl for diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-1h is the 1-hour oral glucose tolerance test, used as a predictor for type 2 diabetes (DM2) with higher sensitivity than other markers.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-1h is a variant of the oral glucose tolerance test where glucose is measured one hour after ingestion, used to detect undiagnosed type 2 diabetes or predict future risk.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-1h is the oral glucose tolerance test with glucose measurement 1 hour after ingestion, preferred for early detection of diabetes risk.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-1h refers to blood glucose measured one hour after glucose ingestion during the oral glucose tolerance test, used as a diagnostic criterion when levels are equal to or greater than 209 mg/dL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0842e3c99a8c4dc96abdcb05ee7fe0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd7841f818e5dcdd6ef3c0c2951cf8a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d8f51fe0c33fe28ed188c855a82a3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688979</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TTGO-2h">
  <data key="d0">TTGO-2h</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TTGO-2h refers to the 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test, a clinical procedure used to assess how the body processes glucose. It involves measuring blood glucose levels exactly two hours after the ingestion of a standardized glucose solution, typically containing 75 grams of glucose. This measurement is a critical component of the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) and is widely recognized as a diagnostic tool for identifying abnormalities in glucose metabolism.

The primary purpose of TTGO-2h is to diagnose diabetes mellitus and impaired glucose tolerance. A blood glucose level of 200 mg/dL or higher at the two-hour mark is considered a diagnostic threshold for diabetes mellitus according to established medical guidelines. In addition to diagnosing diabetes, the test helps identify individuals with impaired glucose tolerance, a condition that may precede the development of type 2 diabetes and is associated with increased metabolic risk.

TTGO-2h is regarded as a standard method in certain clinical settings, particularly when fasting glucose or HbA1c tests are inconclusive. While it is sometimes described as an alternative diagnostic method compared to more routine tests, its role remains important due to its sensitivity in detecting glucose metabolism impairments that might be missed otherwise. The test is performed under controlled conditions to ensure accuracy, and the two-hour measurement is the most critical time point for interpretation. Overall, TTGO-2h serves as a reliable and widely used metric in the evaluation of glucose homeostasis and metabolic health.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-2h refers to the 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test, used to diagnose diabetes mellitus with a cutoff of 200 mg/dl.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-2h is the 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test, commonly used to diagnose impaired glucose tolerance and diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-2h is the standard oral glucose tolerance test with measurement taken two hours after glucose ingestion, used to diagnose diabetes mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-2h is the oral glucose tolerance test with glucose measurement 2 hours after ingestion, used as an alternative diagnostic method.&lt;SEP&gt;TTGO-2h refers to blood glucose measured two hours after glucose ingestion during the oral glucose tolerance test, used as a diagnostic criterion when levels are equal to or greater than 200 mg/dL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0842e3c99a8c4dc96abdcb05ee7fe0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd7841f818e5dcdd6ef3c0c2951cf8a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d8f51fe0c33fe28ed188c855a82a3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688979</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GJ">
  <data key="d0">GJ</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">GJ refers to fasting plasma glucose, a laboratory test that measures blood glucose levels after a period of fasting, typically in the post-absorptive state. It is widely used as a standard diagnostic tool in the initial screening and assessment of glucose metabolism disorders, particularly type 2 diabetes and pre-diabetes. As a diagnostic criterion, GJ helps clinicians evaluate an individual's risk for developing diabetes by identifying abnormal glucose levels that fall within the prediabetic or diabetic ranges.

The test is commonly employed in clinical settings due to its simplicity, accessibility, and cost-effectiveness. It plays a key role in public health strategies for early detection of diabetes, allowing for timely intervention and management. However, despite its widespread use, fasting plasma glucose has been noted to have lower sensitivity compared to other diagnostic methods, such as the one-hour oral glucose tolerance test (TTGO-1h), which may detect glucose abnormalities earlier in the disease process. As a result, while GJ remains a cornerstone in diabetes screening and diagnosis, it may be supplemented with additional tests to improve diagnostic accuracy, especially in high-risk populations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d8f51fe0c33fe28ed188c855a82a3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e72b56afa6bad1b13c937a3ff9bd13d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DM2">
  <data key="d0">DM2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">DM2, or type 2 diabetes mellitus, is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by persistent high blood glucose levels due to insulin resistance and a relative deficiency in insulin secretion. It is marked by the body's reduced ability to respond to insulin effectively, coupled with insufficient insulin production to maintain normal glucose homeostasis. This condition is commonly associated with modifiable and non-modifiable risk factors, including obesity, advancing age, and a family history of diabetes.

DM2 is a major focus in clinical screening and diagnostic practices, with glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) and glucose levels (GJ) serving as primary biomarkers for its detection and monitoring. The assessment of DM2 involves evaluating risk factors, implementing early screening protocols, and establishing accurate diagnoses to manage the disease effectively and prevent complications. Once considered primarily an adult-onset condition, DM2 is increasingly prevalent among children and adolescents, particularly those with obesity and a familial predisposition, signaling a shift in demographic patterns and raising public health concerns.

Overall, DM2 represents a significant and growing health challenge globally, defined by insulin resistance and impaired insulin secretion, and is closely linked to lifestyle and genetic factors. Its diagnosis and management rely on standardized laboratory tests and a comprehensive evaluation of individual risk profiles, underscoring the importance of early intervention and long-term care strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2, or type 2 diabetes mellitus, is a metabolic disorder characterized by insulin resistance and high blood glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2, or Diabetes Mellitus Type 2, is a chronic condition marked by insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency, often linked to obesity and age.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2, or type 2 diabetes mellitus, is a metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels and is the primary condition being screened and diagnosed using GJ and HbA1c.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2 refers to type 2 diabetes mellitus, a condition under assessment in the context of risk factors, screening, and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2 refers to type 2 diabetes mellitus, a metabolic disorder characterized by insulin resistance and reduced insulin secretion, particularly prevalent in children and adolescents with obesity and family history.&lt;SEP&gt;DM2 refers to type 2 diabetes mellitus, a chronic condition characterized by insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd83fee2b80ad551bb282975932751ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e72b56afa6bad1b13c937a3ff9bd13d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3097da75459ebf108182050a889e65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0778abb680b777bec45d5985b757c743</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Metas glicêmicas/Metas no tratamento.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Israel GOH Study">
  <data key="d0">Israel GOH Study</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Israel Study of Glucose Intolerance, Obesity and Hypertension (Israel GOH Study) followed individuals for about 20 years and found elevated risk of DM2 and prediabetes in those with high TTGO-1h values.&lt;SEP&gt;The Israel Study of Glucose Intolerance, Obesity and Hypertension (Israel GOH Study) followed individuals for about 20 years and found elevated risk of DM2 and prediabetes in those with high TTGO-1h values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689016</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RISC Study">
  <data key="d0">RISC Study</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The RISC Study identified that individuals with normal GJ and TTGO-2h but elevated TTGO-1h had greater insulin resistance and progression to glucose intolerance.&lt;SEP&gt;The RISC Study identified that individuals with normal GJ and TTGO-2h but elevated TTGO-1h had greater insulin resistance and progression to glucose intolerance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776689016</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FINDRISC">
  <data key="d0">FINDRISC</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FINDRISC is a risk assessment tool designed to evaluate the likelihood of an individual developing type 2 diabetes, also known as Diabetes Mellitus Type 2. It functions by analyzing a combination of clinical and lifestyle factors to estimate diabetes risk, enabling early identification of individuals who may benefit from preventive interventions. The tool is widely used in primary care and population screening settings to support diabetes prevention strategies.

FINDRISC categorizes an individual's risk level into distinct tiers, typically classified as low, moderate, or high, with particular emphasis on identifying those at high or very high risk. Individuals scoring in the high or very high ranges are considered to have a significantly increased likelihood of developing type 2 diabetes, warranting closer monitoring or lifestyle modification programs. By integrating measurable and self-reported factors such as age, body mass index, physical activity, and dietary habits, FINDRISC provides a practical and non-invasive method for risk stratification. Its consistent application across multiple descriptions highlights its role as a standardized instrument for diabetes risk prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;FINDRISC is a risk assessment tool used to evaluate the likelihood of developing type 2 diabetes based on clinical and lifestyle factors.&lt;SEP&gt;FINDRISC is a risk assessment tool used to identify individuals at high or very high risk for developing Type 2 Diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;FINDRISC is a risk assessment tool used to estimate the likelihood of developing Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 based on clinical and lifestyle factors.&lt;SEP&gt;FINDRISC is a risk assessment tool used to evaluate the likelihood of developing type 2 diabetes, categorized as low, moderate, or high.&lt;SEP&gt;FINDRISC is a risk assessment tool used to estimate an individual's likelihood of developing type 2 diabetes, with high or very high scores indicating increased risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb6ee71a842cf35dd741ee96a80c624b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d8f51fe0c33fe28ed188c855a82a3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea289ce35cdd947df9d56e9e4b5c8566&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e72b56afa6bad1b13c937a3ff9bd13d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3097da75459ebf108182050a889e65</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
