<node id="Pré-Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Pré-Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pré-Diabetes is a metabolic condition characterized by elevated blood glucose levels that do not yet meet the diagnostic threshold for Diabetes Mellitus. It is defined by intermediate hyperglycemia, representing a prediabetic state in which individuals exhibit higher-than-normal blood sugar levels but do not fulfill the criteria for a diabetes diagnosis. This condition is identified through clinical screening and is commonly assessed in population cohorts to evaluate metabolic health and diabetes risk.

Pré-Diabetes is recognized and defined by major health organizations, including the American Diabetes Association and the Brazilian Diabetes Society, which use the term to describe individuals at increased risk of progressing to type 2 diabetes. The condition serves as a critical warning sign, indicating impaired glucose regulation that may stem from insulin resistance or reduced insulin secretion. Early detection through blood testing allows for timely intervention, often involving lifestyle modifications such as improved diet, increased physical activity, and weight management to delay or prevent the onset of full-blown diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pré-diabetes is a condition characterized by elevated blood glucose levels below the threshold for diabetes mellitus diagnosis, assessed in population cohorts.&lt;SEP&gt;Pré-Diabetes is a metabolic condition characterized by elevated blood glucose levels not yet meeting the threshold for Diabetes Mellitus.&lt;SEP&gt;Pré-Diabetes is a condition with elevated blood glucose levels below the threshold for diabetes mellitus, identified through screening.&lt;SEP&gt;Pré-Diabetes is a term used by the American Diabetes Association and the Brazilian Diabetes Society to describe individuals with intermediate hyperglycemia who are at increased risk of developing diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0842e3c99a8c4dc96abdcb05ee7fe0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd7841f818e5dcdd6ef3c0c2951cf8a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff472f3e453a6a19ce0b0606ae3427a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fd2d58abc8f20df1ff7ae241f4cfee7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NHANES III">
  <data key="d0">NHANES III</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">NHANES III is a national health survey that evaluated intraindividual variability of glycemic methods in individuals over 20 years old without diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;NHANES III is a national health survey used to evaluate glycemic variability and diabetes prevalence in individuals over 20 years old.&lt;SEP&gt;NHANES III is a national health survey that evaluated intraindividual variability of glycemic methods in individuals over 20 years old without diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;NHANES III is a national health survey used to evaluate glycemic variability and diabetes prevalence in individuals over 20 years old.&lt;SEP&gt;NHANES III is a U.S. national health survey that provided epidemiological data linking HbA1c levels above 8.0% to increased mortality in elderly diabetic patients.</data>
