<node id="Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease">
  <data key="d0">Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease (MASLD), formerly known as non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), is a liver condition characterized by the accumulation of fat in the liver in individuals who consume little to no alcohol. It is closely linked to metabolic dysfunction, particularly in the context of prediabetes and type 2 diabetes mellitus (DM2). The condition is increasingly recognized as a hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome, with insulin resistance playing a central role in its development and progression.

MASLD is commonly managed in patients with prediabetes or established type 2 diabetes, reflecting its strong association with dysregulated glucose metabolism and obesity. Clinical attention to MASLD is critical because its presence in individuals with type 2 diabetes significantly increases the risk of adverse liver outcomes, including steatohepatitis, fibrosis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma. As such, MASLD is frequently discussed within the framework of metabolic disease management, emphasizing the need for integrated care approaches that address both glycemic control and liver health.

The reclassification from NAFLD to MASLD underscores a shift toward a more accurate, mechanism-based understanding of the disease, highlighting its metabolic underpinnings rather than focusing solely on the absence of alcohol use. This updated terminology aims to improve diagnosis, risk stratification, and treatment by aligning the condition with its primary metabolic drivers. Overall, MASLD represents a growing public health concern due to the rising prevalence of obesity and type 2 diabetes worldwide, necessitating early detection and multidisciplinary management to mitigate long-term complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e35953dbfdebbbab4bf83ec032ccfb7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0f94f216119cfdc3c74eab51c88a823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f017257ae0e7fcc5a0a1be0ddc435fe8</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
