<node id="Diabetes Mellitus Diagnosis">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus Diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Mellitus Diagnosis refers to the clinical process of identifying diabetes, primarily type 2 diabetes, through a combination of symptoms and biochemical markers. The diagnosis is grounded in established clinical criteria, including the presence of symptoms related to hyperglycemia such as excessive thirst, frequent urination, unexplained weight loss, and fatigue. These clinical indicators are typically corroborated with laboratory tests to confirm the condition.

Key diagnostic tools include fasting plasma glucose, which measures blood glucose levels after an overnight fast, and the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), which assesses the body’s ability to metabolize glucose over time. Additionally, glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) is widely used as it reflects average blood glucose levels over the preceding two to three months, providing a more stable and long-term indicator of glycemic control. While HbA1c is a central biomarker, some descriptions reference "GJ" and "TTGO," which appear to be typographical or translational errors likely intended to denote fasting glucose and OGTT, respectively.

The process integrates multiple metabolic markers beyond glucose alone, emphasizing a comprehensive evaluation of the patient’s metabolic state. Although some descriptions specifically highlight type 2 diabetes, the broader diagnostic framework applies to diabetes mellitus as a category, encompassing various forms of the disease when clinical presentation and test results align with established thresholds. Overall, Diabetes Mellitus Diagnosis is a standardized, multi-faceted clinical assessment relying on symptoms, fasting glucose, HbA1c, and glucose tolerance testing to ensure accurate and timely identification of the disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-564f10440969233a515af4974c158c4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c3c6ce19ad6bc3cc3df22e5e10b0702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2977bb9a41689e74de2c536343624744</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688964</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Performance Liquid Chromatography">
  <data key="d0">High Performance Liquid Chromatography</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">High Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) is the standardized laboratory method used for HbA1c analysis, requiring certification by NGSP.&lt;SEP&gt;High Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) is the standardized laboratory method used for HbA1c analysis, requiring certification by NGSP.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-564f10440969233a515af4974c158c4b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688999</data>
