<node id="Glicemia de Jejum">
  <data key="d0">Glicemia de Jejum</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Glicemia de Jejum, also known as fasting blood glucose, is a clinical measurement used to assess blood glucose levels after a period of fasting, typically lasting at least 8 hours. This test is widely employed as a primary screening tool for type 2 diabetes, offering a reliable and accessible method for initial detection of glucose metabolism abnormalities. It plays a critical role in identifying individuals who may require further diagnostic evaluation or intervention.

The measurement is a key diagnostic criterion for both diabetes mellitus and prediabetes. A fasting blood glucose level of ≥126 mg/dl is the established threshold for diagnosing diabetes mellitus, based on standardized clinical guidelines. In addition to confirming diabetes, Glicemia de Jejum helps identify individuals with prediabetic conditions, allowing for early lifestyle or medical interventions to prevent or delay disease progression. Repeated across multiple sources, the consistency in defining the cutoff value and diagnostic purpose underscores its importance in clinical practice.

Due to its simplicity, cost-effectiveness, and reproducibility, Glicemia de Jejum remains one of the most commonly used tests in routine health assessments and diabetes screening programs. It provides essential data for healthcare providers to evaluate metabolic health and make informed decisions regarding patient care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff472f3e453a6a19ce0b0606ae3427a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fd2d58abc8f20df1ff7ae241f4cfee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3097da75459ebf108182050a889e65</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Diagnostico e Classificação/Diagnóstico de diabetes mellitus.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1776688944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
