<edge source="Best" target="Banting">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Banting and Best collaborated extensively on the development, preparation, and testing of pancreatic extracts for the treatment of diabetes, a groundbreaking endeavor in medical science during the early 1920s. Their joint research focused on isolating and refining insulin from animal pancreases, with a significant portion of their experimental work conducted on diabetic dogs to evaluate the extract's efficacy in regulating blood sugar levels. These animal trials were critical in demonstrating the therapeutic potential of insulin, laying the foundation for its eventual use in humans.

A pivotal moment in their collaboration occurred on 23 November 1921, when Banting and Best injected each other with a small dose of the pancreatic extract to test its safety. The absence of harmful effects in themselves provided crucial preliminary evidence that the substance was safe for human use, marking a key step toward clinical application. This act underscored their commitment to the research and their willingness to take personal risks in advancing medical science. Their close working relationship extended beyond the laboratory, culminating in a notable moment just before the first human trial of insulin, where their partnership played a central role in initiating a new era in diabetes treatment. Through their combined efforts, Banting and Best made an indispensable contribution to the discovery and development of insulin as a life-saving therapy.</data>
  <data key="d9">joint research,research partnership,safety testing,scientific collaboration,self-experimentation,self-testing,shared discovery</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cfc036e7cdb0e2bea999e115a813d0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b1b0acb290f4189442d14cf5ca48947&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c04d944f188525956166c3081e7e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7dbe6650faedb4c8fae58ee3791670&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bf43b932b33f125e5a5e679dcb50d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e86a3a1d4228f2b3b5e588cb46fdd3cc</data>
